# ---------------- 5.1 Energy flow ----------------
E('5.1-01','Energy from the Sun','Sunlight is the energy source for most food chains. Photosynthesis transfers light energy into chemical energy stored in organic substances such as sugars and starch.',
 'When organisms feed, some of that chemical energy passes along the food chain. Respiration and other activities transfer energy to the surroundings as heat, so an ecosystem needs a continuing supply of energy from the Sun.',
 'Energy flows through an ecosystem in one direction: from the Sun into producers, along the food chain by feeding, and out to the surroundings as heat. Matter such as carbon and nitrogen is recycled, but the heat is not recycled by plants into the food chain. This is why the Sun must keep supplying energy.\n\nProducers respire as well as photosynthesise, so even a grass plant transfers some energy to the surroundings as heat. A mammal such as a lion keeps its body warmer than its surroundings and so transfers a great deal of heat. When we say that energy is "lost" from a food chain, we mean it is no longer available to the next feeder. It has not been destroyed.\n\nA good description of energy flow names three stages in order: light energy becomes chemical energy in photosynthesis, feeding passes some chemical energy on, and respiration and activity transfer energy to the surroundings as heat.',loc='46.2 Energy Flow through Ecosystems')
C('5.1-01','PROC','Describe the route of sunlight energy through a green plant and a herbivore to the environment.','Photosynthesis transfers light energy into chemical energy in the plant\'s organic material. Some of this passes to the herbivore when it feeds. Respiration and activity then transfer energy to the environment as heat.',['1 mark each for any two of these steps: photosynthesis transfers light energy into chemical energy in the plant; feeding passes some of that chemical energy to the herbivore; respiration and activity transfer energy to the surroundings as heat.','Both marks need the steps in the correct order, so a route given in the wrong order gains at most 1 mark.'])
D('5.1-02','Producer','A producer is an organism that makes its own organic nutrients, usually using energy from sunlight, through photosynthesis.',
 'Producers supply organic material for food chains. They also respire and use some of that material themselves.',
 'A grass plant makes sugars from carbon dioxide and water using light energy. It does not obtain its organic food by absorbing it from the soil. Mineral ions such as nitrate are nutrients in a different sense: they are needed to build proteins but do not supply energy.\n\nProducers include trees, grasses, pond algae and the microscopic phytoplankton of the sea. Off the coast of Namibia, the cold, nutrient-rich water of the Benguela upwelling supports large numbers of phytoplankton. These producers are the base of food chains that lead to fish, seabirds and Cape fur seals.\n\nA common mistake is to call any organism at the bottom of a diagram a producer. Check how it gets its food. A producer makes its food by photosynthesis, while a fungus on a dead leaf, even at the start of a decay chain, is a decomposer.',['a producer makes its own organic nutrients','it usually uses light energy, through photosynthesis'])
D('5.1-03','Consumer','A consumer is an organism that gets its energy by feeding on other organisms.',
 'A consumer obtains organic material by feeding. It does not make its whole organic food supply from inorganic substances by photosynthesis.',
 'A grasshopper eating grass is a primary consumer. A lizard eating the grasshopper is a secondary consumer. On the savanna, a springbok eating grass is a primary consumer and a cheetah that catches the springbok is a secondary consumer. The level of a consumer depends on what it eats, not on how large it is.\n\nAn organism can occupy more than one trophic level in a food web if it eats different foods. A jackal that eats fruit is a primary consumer at that moment, and one that eats a mouse is a secondary consumer.',['a consumer gets its energy by feeding','what it feeds on is other organisms'])
D('5.1-04','Food chain','A food chain shows the transfer of energy from one organism to the next, beginning with a producer.',
 'Arrows point from the food organism towards the feeder that receives its energy, not from a predator towards the prey it attacks.',
 'If grass is eaten by a grasshopper, which is eaten by a lizard, the food chain is grass → grasshopper → lizard. Each arrow means "energy passes to". A sea food chain off Namibia could be phytoplankton → zooplankton → anchovy → Cape fur seal.\n\nA chain is a simplified route. It leaves out the other foods each organism eats and the decomposers that act on dead material, so it shows one pathway through an ecosystem and not the whole of it.',['a food chain shows the transfer of energy from one organism to the next','the chain begins with a producer'])
E('5.1-05','Constructing a supported food chain','A food chain built from feeding observations must begin with a producer, follow what actually eats what, and have arrows pointing towards the consumer.',
 'Two organisms living in the same habitat are not necessarily linked in a chain. Evidence that one feeds on the other is needed.',
 'Start by finding the photosynthetic organism. Then find what eats it, and then what eats that consumer. For example, from the observations that beetle larvae eat grass roots and a lizard eats the larvae, the chain is grass → beetle larva → lizard.\n\nIf an observation mentions an organism that does not link to the others, leave it out. A beetle that feeds on dead wood is a detritus feeder and belongs to a different chain. If two different animals both eat the same food, you can draw two valid chains, so always use only the links the evidence supports.',loc='46.2 Energy Flow through Ecosystems')
C('5.1-05','APP','Suggest a three-member food chain from these observations: beetle larvae eat grass roots, a small lizard eats the larvae, and another beetle feeds on dead wood. Show the direction of energy transfer.','Grass → beetle larva → lizard.',['1 mark: gives the chain in this order, grass then beetle larva then lizard, leaving out the dead-wood beetle because it does not feed on the others.','1 mark: each arrow points from the organism eaten to the feeder, because the arrows show the direction of energy transfer.'])
D('5.1-06','Food web','A food web is a network of interconnected food chains.',
 'One organism may have several food sources and several predators, linking different energy-transfer routes.',
 'A bird that eats both seeds and insects joins a producer-to-consumer chain to another consumer chain. If one food source is removed, the effect passes through several links. The result depends on whether the animal can switch to other foods and on how much other animals compete for them.\n\nWhen you read a web, follow the arrows. The arrows show energy transfer, so an organism drawn near the top of a diagram is not necessarily a top consumer. Count the links from the producer to find its level.',['a food web is made up of food chains','the chains are interconnected, forming a network'])
D('5.1-07','Herbivore','A herbivore is an organism that gets its energy by eating plants.',
 'In a chain that begins with a green plant, the herbivore is the primary consumer.',
 '"Herbivore" describes the food source and not the size of the animal. A large antelope and a tiny leaf-eating insect are both herbivores. Plants contain chemical energy, but the herbivore must still digest the food and absorb the products before its cells can use them.',['a herbivore gets its energy by eating','its food is plants'])
D('5.1-08','Carnivore','A carnivore is an animal that gets its energy by eating other animals.',
 'A carnivore can occupy different consumer levels depending on what its prey eats.',
 'A spider that eats a plant-eating insect is a secondary consumer in that chain. A bird that eats the spider is a tertiary consumer. The level depends on how many feeding steps lie between the animal and the producer, so the biggest animal in a diagram is not automatically a carnivore at the top level. Animals that eat both plants and animals are omnivores.',['a carnivore is an animal that gets its energy by eating','its food is other animals'])
D('5.1-09','Decomposer','A decomposer is an organism that gets its energy from dead or waste organic material.',
 'Many bacteria and fungi are decomposers. They release soluble products and mineral ions, and their respiration transfers carbon back to the environment as carbon dioxide.',
 'Decomposers do not make food from sunlight. A fungus releases enzymes onto dead leaves, digests them outside its cells and absorbs the products. This is why dead material does not pile up in an ecosystem. Decomposers act on material from every trophic level, including dead plants, dead animals, faeces and leaf litter.\n\nAnimals such as beetles and earthworms that eat dead material speed up decay by breaking it into smaller pieces, but bacteria and fungi do most of the chemical breakdown that recycles mineral ions.',['a decomposer gets its energy from organic material','the material it uses is dead or waste material'])
C('5.1-09','MISCON','A poster shows a decay chain with a fungus growing on dead leaves drawn first, at the bottom of the diagram. Learner P says the fungus is a producer because it starts the chain. Learner Q says it is a decomposer. Explain which learner is correct.','Learner Q is correct. Being first or at the bottom of a diagram does not make an organism a producer; a producer makes organic food by photosynthesis, and a fungus cannot photosynthesise. The fungus gets its energy and organic material from the dead leaves, so it is a decomposer.',['1 mark: names the error in P\'s idea: a place at the start or bottom of a diagram does not make an organism a producer, because a producer makes organic food by photosynthesis and the fungus cannot.','1 mark: chooses decomposer instead, as Q says, because the fungus obtains its energy and organic material from the dead leaves.'])
D('5.1-10','Trophic level','Trophic level is the position of an organism in a food chain, food web, pyramid of numbers or pyramid of biomass.',
 'Producers form the first level, followed by primary, secondary and further consumers along a stated feeding route.',
 'An omnivore can occupy different levels in different chains. The position of a bar in a pyramid shows the feeding level and has nothing to do with the physical height of the organisms. Trace the feeding links before you name a level, and do not confuse primary production (the producers) with primary consumption (the herbivores).',['trophic level is the position of an organism in a food chain, food web or pyramid','the position is counted in feeding steps from the producers, which form the first level'],
 ans='Trophic level is the position of an organism in a food chain, food web, pyramid of numbers or pyramid of biomass. It is counted in feeding steps from the producers, which form the first level.')
E('5.1-11','Naming trophic positions','Producer, primary consumer, secondary consumer, tertiary consumer and quaternary consumer identify successive positions along a food chain.',
 'The prefix counts consumer steps after the producer: a quaternary consumer is the fourth consumer and sits at the fifth trophic level.',
 'In the chain grass → caterpillar → predatory beetle → lizard → hawk, the caterpillar is the primary consumer, the beetle is secondary, the lizard is tertiary and the hawk is quaternary. Most natural chains are shorter than this.\n\nPyramids of numbers count the organisms at each level. A pyramid of numbers can be an odd shape, for example when one large tree supports thousands of insects, because the count ignores body size. A pyramid of biomass shows the dry mass at each level, so it takes size into account.',loc='46.2 Energy Flow through Ecosystems')
C('5.1-11','FEATURE','Identify the producer and the secondary consumer in this chain: grass → caterpillar → predatory beetle → lizard.','Producer: grass. Secondary consumer: predatory beetle.',['1 mark: names grass as the producer.','1 mark: names the predatory beetle as the secondary consumer.'])
E('5.1-12','Transfer between trophic levels','Food transfers chemical energy to the next consumer, but some material is uneaten or egested, and some energy is used in respiration and transferred to the surroundings as heat.',
 'The energy retained in new consumer biomass is therefore smaller than the energy in the food that was available.',
 'There are three routes for the energy in food. Some is egested as faeces, and this material goes on to decomposers. Some is used in respiration to power movement, growth and keeping warm, and ends up as heat. Only the remainder is stored in new biomass, which is what the next feeder can eat.\n\nWorked example. A consumer eats food containing 400 kJ. It egests 120 kJ and uses 240 kJ in respiration. The energy left for new biomass is 400 − 120 − 240 = 40 kJ, which is 10% of the energy eaten. The percentage that reaches the next level varies from one food chain to another. Ten per cent is a useful approximation, not a fixed law.',loc='46.2 Energy Flow through Ecosystems')
C('5.1-12','PROC','Explain why eating plant material does not transfer all its energy into new herbivore biomass.','Some of the plant material is not eaten, or is egested undigested. Of the energy that is absorbed, some is used in respiration and activity and leaves as heat, so less is left in new biomass.',['1 mark: some plant material is not eaten, or is egested undigested, so its energy is not absorbed.','1 mark: some of the absorbed energy is used in respiration and activity and transferred as heat, so less is left for new biomass. The two points can be given in any order.'])
C('5.1-12','APP','Describe the energy budget of a consumer by calculating the energy that remains in new biomass when 500 kJ is eaten, 150 kJ is egested and 300 kJ is transferred to the surroundings through respiration, with no other changes.','500 − 150 − 300 = 50 kJ remains in new biomass. Most of the energy eaten, 450 kJ, leaves by egestion and respiration, so only 10% is stored.',['1 mark: subtracts both given losses from the energy eaten, 500 − 150 − 300 = 50 kJ, with the unit.','1 mark: describes the budget: 450 of the 500 kJ leaves by egestion and respiration, so only a small fraction (10%) is stored.'])
E('5.1-13','Limits to chain length','At each trophic transfer less energy remains in new biomass for the next consumer level.',
 'This limits how much biomass and how many top consumers a producer base can support, so food chains usually have fewer than five trophic levels.',
 'Chain length is not set by a rule against a fifth level. It is set by the energy available at the base, how efficiently energy passes between levels, and how much energy a top consumer needs. A top predator with a large territory, such as a lion, can draw on a broad base of producers through many herbivores.\n\nWorked example. A base supplies 1000 kJ per year and each transfer keeps 20%. The next levels receive 200 kJ, then 40 kJ, then 8 kJ. If a population of consumers needs 30 kJ per year to survive, the primary and secondary consumer levels (200 kJ and 40 kJ) can be supported but a tertiary consumer level (8 kJ) cannot. Real systems have different percentages, so calculations always use the fraction the question states.',loc='46.2 Energy Flow through Ecosystems')
C('5.1-13','CHAIN','Explain why food chains usually have fewer than five trophic levels.','Less energy is available at each transfer because of respiration and heat loss and because some material is uneaten or egested. After a few transfers, too little energy is left to support another population at the top of a long chain.',['1 mark: less energy is passed on at each transfer, because of respiration, heat loss and material that is uneaten or egested.','1 mark: after a few transfers too little energy is left to support another population, and this effect limits the chain length.'])
C('5.1-13','APP','A producer base provides 1000 kJ per year and each transfer to the next level keeps 20% of the energy. A consumer population needs at least 30 kJ per year. Explain why the first two consumer levels could be supported but the third could not.','The energy available is 200 kJ, then 40 kJ, then 8 kJ. The first two are above 30 kJ and the third is below it, so the third level cannot be supported.',['1 mark: calculates the energy at each consumer level from the given 20%: 200 kJ, 40 kJ and 8 kJ.','1 mark: 200 kJ and 40 kJ are above the 30 kJ needed but 8 kJ is below it, so the third level cannot be supported.'])
E('5.1-14','Biomass pyramids','A pyramid of biomass shows the dry mass of living material at each trophic level, with the width of each bar proportional to dry mass per unit area or volume.',
 'Most land pyramids narrow upwards. A pyramid of biomass is a snapshot of standing mass, which is not the same as the rate of energy flow, and it can be inverted where producers reproduce very quickly.',
 'Read the labels and units before comparing bar widths. A wide producer bar shows mass, not a large number of plants. If producers have 800 g/m² and primary consumers have 80 g/m², the producers have ten times the mass. This tells you the mass at that moment. It does not show the percentage of energy passed on in a year.\n\nIn the sea, an inverted pyramid can occur. Phytoplankton have a small mass at any moment because they reproduce and are eaten within days, yet they support a larger standing mass of zooplankton and fish.',loc='46.2 Energy Flow through Ecosystems')
C('5.1-14','APP','Fig. VIS-6116-5.1-14 is a pyramid of biomass for a grassland. Its bars show dry mass per square metre of 800 g for producers, 80 g for primary consumers, 12 g for secondary consumers and 2 g for tertiary consumers. Describe the pattern in the pyramid and state one limit on what it shows.','The biomass falls at each level from 800 g/m² for producers to 2 g/m² for tertiary consumers, so the pyramid narrows strongly upwards. It is a snapshot of standing mass, so it does not by itself show how much energy is transferred in a year.',['1 mark: biomass decreases at every level, from the given 800 g/m² for producers to 2 g/m² for tertiary consumers.','1 mark: the pyramid shows standing mass at one moment, so it does not show the rate or efficiency of energy transfer; accept another valid limit, such as that it describes one site at one time.'],True)
E('5.1-15','Food supply and trophic efficiency','Eating suitable crop plants directly avoids an extra trophic transfer through livestock, with its respiratory and material losses.',
 'Feeding edible crops to animals usually leaves less of their energy available as human food, although land suitability, nutrition and the use of inedible material also matter.',
 'Energy is lost at every level. If people eat maize directly, they gain from the first transfer only. If the maize is fed to cattle and people then eat the beef, there is an extra transfer with respiration, heat and uneaten material. So the same land can feed more people when people eat crops directly.\n\nThere are good reasons to keep livestock as well. In much of Namibia, rainfall is too low and too unreliable for crops, but cattle, goats and sheep can turn the rangeland vegetation, which people cannot digest, into meat and milk. Animal products also supply good-quality protein and some minerals. Which route is better depends on the land, the crop and the needs of the people.',loc='46.2 Energy Flow through Ecosystems')
C('5.1-15','CHAIN','Explain why eating suitable crop plants directly is usually more energy-efficient than feeding those crops to livestock and then eating the livestock.','The extra feeding step through an animal loses energy in respiration and in uneaten or egested material. Avoiding that step leaves more of the crop\'s energy available as food for people.',['1 mark: an extra trophic transfer through livestock loses energy as heat in respiration and in uneaten or egested material.','1 mark: eating the crop directly avoids that loss, so the effect is that more of the crop energy reaches people.'])
C('5.1-15','APP','A harvest of an edible crop provides 9000 MJ of food energy if people eat it directly. If the crop is instead fed to livestock and the livestock are eaten, it provides only 900 MJ. Explain why the direct route is more efficient.','9000 ÷ 900 = 10, so ten times as much food energy is available by the direct route. The livestock route has an extra trophic level, where energy is transferred as heat in respiration and lost in material that is not eaten or is egested.',['1 mark: compares the given figures, 9000 ÷ 900 = 10, so ten times as much food energy is available directly.','1 mark: the livestock route adds a feeding step in which energy is lost as heat in respiration and in uneaten or egested material, because animals use most of the energy they eat.'])

# ---------------- 5.2 Nitrogen and carbon cycles ----------------
E('5.2-01','Overview of the cycles','Nitrogen moves through air, soil and organisms. Fixation makes atmospheric nitrogen available in compounds, plants take up mineral nitrogen, and feeding passes organic nitrogen to animals.',
 'Decomposition returns nitrogen compounds from dead and waste material, nitrification forms nitrates in oxygenated conditions, and denitrification returns nitrogen gas to the air in oxygen-poor conditions.',
 'Plants need nitrogen to make amino acids and proteins. Nitrogen gas makes up about four-fifths of the air, but plants cannot use it directly. Microorganisms carry out the key conversions that make nitrogen usable and return it to the air.\n\nFollow the chemical form at each stage. Nitrogen in a protein is not yet nitrate, and nitrogen gas is not yet ammonium. Decomposition and the other cycles move matter back into reusable forms. They do not recycle energy.\n\nThe later sections follow carbon in the same way, through photosynthesis, feeding, respiration, decomposition, fossilisation and combustion.',loc='46.3 Biogeochemical Cycles')
C('5.2-01','PROC','Describe the main route of nitrogen from soil nitrate into an animal and back to the soil.','A plant absorbs nitrate through its roots and uses the nitrogen to make proteins, and an animal obtains them by eating the plant. Decomposers then break down dead and waste material, which returns nitrogen compounds to the soil.',['1 mark: nitrogen absorbed by the plant as nitrate reaches the animal in proteins when it eats the plant.','1 mark: decomposers then break down dead and waste material, so nitrogen compounds return to the soil.'])
C('5.2-01','MECH','Describe how nitrogen is cycled between the air, the soil and living organisms.','Nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert nitrogen gas from the air into nitrogen compounds. Plants take up nitrate and animals obtain nitrogen compounds by feeding on plants. Decomposers break down dead and waste material to release ammonium, and nitrifying bacteria convert ammonium through nitrite to nitrate. Denitrifying bacteria in oxygen-poor soil convert nitrate to nitrogen gas, which returns to the air.',['1 mark each for any four of these five points.','Nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert nitrogen gas from the air into nitrogen compounds.','Plants take up nitrate to make proteins, and animals then obtain the nitrogen by feeding.','Decomposers break down dead and waste material, which releases ammonium.','Nitrifying bacteria convert ammonium through nitrite to nitrate, which needs oxygen.','Denitrifying bacteria convert nitrate to nitrogen gas in oxygen-poor soil, so nitrogen returns to the air.'])
nitrogen=[
 ('02','Decomposition and ammonium','Decomposer microorganisms break down nitrogen-containing dead and waste organic material and release ammonium compounds.','This supplies a form of nitrogen that other bacteria can convert further.','Their enzymes act on organic material such as proteins and urea. Decomposition is not the same as taking nitrogen gas from the air, which is fixation. Decomposers work faster in warm, moist, oxygenated soil. Waterlogged or very dry soil slows them down.','State the role of decomposer microorganisms in the nitrogen cycle.','They break down dead and waste organic material containing nitrogen and release ammonium compounds.','1 mark for the point that decomposers release ammonium compounds from dead and waste material containing nitrogen.'),
 ('03','Nitrification','Nitrifying microorganisms convert ammonium compounds into nitrites and then nitrates.','Oxygen is needed for this conversion, and it makes nitrate available for plants to take up.','Keep nitrification separate from fixation. In nitrification the nitrogen is already combined in ammonium, not in nitrogen gas. The nitrate that results can be absorbed by roots, but it is also very soluble and can be washed out of the soil by rain, which is called leaching.','State the role of nitrifying microorganisms.','They convert ammonium compounds, through nitrite, into nitrate.','1 mark for the point that nitrifying bacteria convert ammonium to nitrate (through nitrite).'),
 ('04','Nitrogen fixation','Nitrogen-fixing microorganisms convert atmospheric nitrogen gas into nitrogen compounds that can enter living things.','Some live freely in the soil, and others live in root nodules of legumes such as peas, beans and cowpeas.','The bacteria in root nodules receive sugars from the legume, and the legume receives nitrogen compounds from the bacteria. Cowpeas, which are grown in northern Namibia, are an example of a legume that can add usable nitrogen to the soil. The amount depends on healthy nodules and suitable conditions.\n\nThe plant does not absorb nitrogen gas as if it were nitrate. The nitrogen reaches the next crop mainly when the legume residues decompose in the soil.','State the role of nitrogen-fixing microorganisms.','They convert nitrogen gas from the air into nitrogen compounds that plants can use.','1 mark for the point that nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert nitrogen gas from the air into nitrogen compounds.'),
 ('05','Denitrification','Denitrifying microorganisms convert nitrate to nitrogen gas in oxygen-poor conditions.','This returns nitrogen to the atmosphere and can reduce the nitrate available to plants.','Denitrification is not nitrification in reverse. It happens in soil with little oxygen, such as soil that is waterlogged after heavy rain. In those conditions, denitrifying bacteria use nitrate in place of oxygen, and nitrogen gas escapes into the air. For a farmer, this means a waterlogged field can lose nitrate that was meant for the crop.','State the role of denitrifying microorganisms.','They convert nitrate to nitrogen gas when oxygen is in short supply.','1 mark for the point that denitrifying bacteria convert nitrate to nitrogen gas when oxygen is short.')]
for n,h,c1,c2,more,p,a,g in nitrogen:
 o='5.2-'+n;E(o,h,c1,c2,more,loc='46.3 Biogeochemical Cycles');C(o,'FEATURE',p,a,[g])
carbon=[
 ('06','Photosynthesis and carbon','Photosynthesis removes carbon dioxide from the air or water and incorporates its carbon into organic compounds in producers.','That carbon can become sugars, starch, cellulose and other biological material.','The carbon comes from carbon dioxide, and light supplies the energy. Plants also respire, so whether a plant takes in or gives out carbon dioxide overall depends on the balance of photosynthesis and respiration. In bright light, a leaf takes in more carbon dioxide than it releases. In the dark, only respiration continues.','Describe the role of photosynthesis in the carbon cycle.','Producers take in carbon dioxide. Photosynthesis then builds its carbon into organic substances, so carbon moves from the environment into living material.','1 mark: producers take in carbon dioxide from the air or water.','1 mark: photosynthesis then builds the carbon into organic substances such as glucose, so carbon moves into living material.'),
 ('07','Respiration and carbon','Respiration of organic substances can release carbon dioxide to the environment from producers, consumers and decomposers.','This returns carbon from living material to the pool that photosynthesis draws on.','Respiration is not just breathing in animals. Plants respire in light and in darkness, and so do fungi and bacteria. The breakdown of glucose in aerobic respiration releases energy and produces carbon dioxide and water.','Describe the role of respiration in the carbon cycle.','Producers, consumers and decomposers break down organic substances in respiration. The carbon is then released as carbon dioxide to the environment.','1 mark: producers, consumers and decomposers break down organic substances such as glucose in respiration.','1 mark: the carbon is then released as carbon dioxide, so it returns to the air or water.'),
 ('08','Feeding and carbon','Feeding transfers carbon-containing organic material from one organism to another.','Some of that material is absorbed and built into the consumer\'s body, while some is egested or respired.','A feeding arrow shows the transfer of matter as well as energy. Carbon that enters an animal is not all kept. Some becomes body tissue, some is respired as carbon dioxide and some leaves in faeces. Faeces and dead animals then pass carbon to decomposers.','Describe the role of feeding in the carbon cycle.','A consumer eats carbon-containing organic material. Some of it is then absorbed and built into its own tissues, so carbon passes from one organism to another.','1 mark: a consumer eats organic material that contains carbon.','1 mark: some of it is then absorbed and built into the consumer\'s own tissues, so carbon passes from one organism to another.'),
 ('09','Decomposition and carbon dioxide','Decomposers process dead and waste organic material, using some of its carbon compounds in respiration.','Aerobic decomposition releases carbon dioxide, returning carbon to the environment, while some carbon is built into decomposer bodies or remains in the soil.','Decomposition slows when the temperature is low, when the material is too dry or waterlogged, or when oxygen is short. A fallen log does not turn into gas at once. It breaks down gradually, and its carbon moves into the air, into fungi and bacteria, and into the soil.','Describe the role of decomposition in returning carbon to the environment.','Decomposers break down dead and waste organic material. Their respiration then releases some of its carbon as carbon dioxide.','1 mark: decomposers break down dead and waste organic material.','1 mark: their respiration then releases carbon from it as carbon dioxide, so carbon returns to the environment.'),
 ('10','Fossilisation and carbon','In particular burial and preservation conditions, some ancient organic carbon avoids complete decomposition and enters long-term geological stores.','Over geological time this can form fossil fuels, removing that carbon from short-term biological exchange until it is extracted and burnt.','Most dead organisms decompose and never become fuel. Fossil fuels formed from remains that were buried without much oxygen and then changed over millions of years. Coal formed mainly from plant material, and oil and natural gas formed mainly from tiny marine organisms.','Describe the role of fossilisation in the carbon cycle.','Some buried dead material does not decompose completely. Over millions of years its carbon then forms long-term stores such as coal, oil and gas.','1 mark: some dead organisms are buried and do not decompose completely.','1 mark: over millions of years their carbon then forms fossil fuels such as coal, oil and gas, so it is stored for a very long time.'),
 ('11','Combustion and carbon','Combustion of carbon-containing fuels releases carbon dioxide because the carbon is oxidised.','Burning fossil fuels moves carbon from long-term stores into the atmosphere on a much shorter timescale.','Complete combustion uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide and water. Wood, charcoal, coal and petrol all release carbon dioxide when they burn. Burning does not create carbon from nothing. It moves carbon that was stored for a long time into the air.','Describe the role of combustion in the carbon cycle.','A fuel containing carbon is burnt. Carbon dioxide is then released, so carbon moves from the fuel store to the atmosphere.','1 mark: a fuel that contains carbon is burnt, using oxygen.','1 mark: carbon dioxide is then released, so carbon moves from the fuel store into the atmosphere.')]
for n,h,c1,c2,more,p,a,g1,g2 in carbon:
 o='5.2-'+n;E(o,h,c1,c2,more,loc='46.3 Biogeochemical Cycles');C(o,'PROC',p,a,[g1,g2])
E('5.2-12','Fossil fuels and the balance of gases','Burning fossil fuels uses oxygen and releases carbon dioxide, transferring carbon from a long-term store into the air.',
 'If the carbon dioxide released is more than the amount removed, the concentration in the atmosphere rises. The effect depends on the whole balance and not on one source.',
 'Burning uses up oxygen, but the air holds so much oxygen that the overall change in atmospheric oxygen is very small. The important change is the extra carbon dioxide. Compare the rates of release, uptake and storage when you judge the effect on the atmosphere.\n\nWorked example. A model atmosphere was in balance until fuel burning added 9 carbon units each year. Extra uptake by plants and the sea now removes 6 units a year. The net gain is 9 − 6 = +3 units a year, so the carbon in the air keeps increasing, only more slowly. The system is in balance only when uptake equals release.',loc='46.3 Biogeochemical Cycles')
C('5.2-12','CHAIN','Discuss three linked effects of burning fossil fuels: on oxygen use, on carbon dioxide release and on the carbon stored in fuels.','Burning uses oxygen from the air. It releases carbon dioxide because the carbon in the fuel is oxidised. Carbon moves from a long-term store in the fuel into the atmosphere, so atmospheric carbon dioxide rises if removal does not match release.',['1 mark: burning uses up oxygen from the air.','1 mark: carbon dioxide is released because the carbon in the fuel is oxidised.','1 mark: carbon moves from a long-term store into the air, so the effect is that atmospheric carbon dioxide increases if uptake does not match release.'])
C('5.2-12','APP','A model atmosphere was in balance before fuel burning began. Burning now adds 9 carbon units a year, and natural uptake has risen by 6 units a year in response. Suggest the annual net change and why extra uptake alone does not give a balanced system.','The net change is 9 − 6 = +3 units a year. Uptake has risen but is still less than the added release, so carbon keeps accumulating in the atmosphere.',['1 mark: calculates the net change from the given figures, 9 − 6 = an increase of 3 units a year.','1 mark: uptake (6) is still less than the added release (9), so carbon keeps building up in the air and the system is not balanced.'])
E('5.2-13','Forest removal and carbon balance','Deforestation reduces the uptake of carbon dioxide by photosynthesis and can release stored carbon when trees are burnt or decay.',
 'Cleared land also loses habitat and soil protection. The net effect on the air depends on how much carbon is released and how much new growth takes up.',
 'Fewer trees means less photosynthesis, so less oxygen is released and less carbon dioxide is removed from the air. Burning or decay of the felled trees uses oxygen and releases carbon dioxide. As with fossil fuels, the oxygen used is a very small fraction of the oxygen in the air, so the percentage change in atmospheric oxygen is tiny.\n\nA forest holds carbon in three places: the standing trees, the soil and the dead material on the ground. Removing trees reduces the current carbon store, and it reduces the amount that will be taken up each year in future.\n\nPlanting seedlings starts to replace the uptake, but a young plantation takes many years to match the carbon store, the habitat and the soil conditions of an old forest. Planting one seedling is not an immediate equal replacement for a felled tree.',loc='46.3 Biogeochemical Cycles')
C('5.2-13','CHAIN','Discuss three linked effects on carbon dioxide of removing and burning a forest.','Photosynthetic uptake of carbon dioxide falls because there are fewer trees. Burning releases the stored carbon as carbon dioxide because the organic material is oxidised. Less uptake and more release together raise the carbon dioxide concentration in the atmosphere.',['1 mark: with fewer trees, less carbon dioxide is taken up by photosynthesis.','1 mark: burning oxidises the wood, which releases its stored carbon as carbon dioxide.','1 mark: less uptake and more release together have the effect of increasing carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.'])
C('5.2-13','APP','A cleared forest held 100 carbon units. New seedlings planted there hold 2 units and take up 1 unit a year. Suggest why planting them does not immediately replace the carbon that was removed.','The seedlings hold only 2 of the 100 units that were removed. Uptake of 1 unit a year is a rate, so the store builds up only over many years of growth.',['1 mark: the seedlings hold only 2 of the 100 units removed, so most of this carbon store is still missing.','1 mark: because uptake is only 1 unit a year, the store rebuilds only over many years of growth.'])
E('5.2-14','Long-term increase in carbon dioxide','A sustained increase in atmospheric carbon dioxide strengthens heat retention and can change the climate, affecting temperature, rainfall and the movement of carbon through living things.',
 'Changing conditions can alter photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition and carbon storage, so any feedback needs its direction and its conditions stated.',
 'Warmer conditions can speed up decomposition if there is enough water. Drought can slow decomposers and photosynthesis. More carbon dioxide does not guarantee more plant growth if water, mineral ions or another factor is limiting. Namibia has a dry climate with variable rainfall, so water is often the factor that limits plant growth.\n\nOther effects follow from warming. Rainfall patterns change, so some regions have more droughts and others more floods. Melting ice and the expansion of warmer sea water raise sea levels. Species may lose the conditions they need where they live.',loc='46.3 Biogeochemical Cycles')
C('5.2-14','CHAIN','Discuss three linked possible effects of a long-term increase in atmospheric carbon dioxide.','More carbon dioxide increases heat retention, so the average temperature rises. Warming changes rainfall patterns, so some areas have more droughts and others more floods. Melting ice and the expansion of warmer sea water raise sea levels.',['1 mark for each of any three effects, each linked to the extra carbon dioxide or to the effect before it.','Creditable effects include: more heat is retained, so the average temperature increases; rainfall patterns change, which brings more droughts or floods in some places; sea levels rise as ice melts and warmer water expands; warmer, moist soil decomposes faster, which releases more carbon dioxide; species lose suitable conditions, so some become endangered.'])
C('5.2-14','APP','In a model, warm soil releases more carbon dioxide when it is moist but almost none after severe drying. Suggest why warming alone cannot decide whether the soil releases more carbon dioxide.','Decomposers need water as well as warmth. When the soil is severely dry their activity almost stops, so moisture, not temperature alone, decides whether warming leads to more carbon dioxide release.',['1 mark: decomposers need water as well as warmth, so in this dry soil their activity almost stops.','1 mark: whichever factor is in short supply limits decomposition, so warming increases carbon dioxide release only when there is enough water.'])

# ---------------- 5.3 Populations ----------------
D('5.3-01','Population','A population is a group of organisms of one species that live in the same area at the same time.',
 'A count needs its species, area and time to be stated. Changing any of them changes which population is being described.',
 'All the springbok in Etosha National Park during one survey year form a population. If you count several species together, you are describing part of a community instead. Counting the animals around one waterhole does not give the whole population of the park, because the animals are spread over a large area.',['a population is a group of organisms of one species','they live in the same area at the same time'],head='What a population is')
E('5.3-02','Population growth factors','Births and immigration add individuals to a population. Deaths and emigration remove them.',
 'Food, water, space, disease, predation and other conditions change these rates and so affect net population growth.',
 'A population can grow without any births if immigration is high enough. A population with a high birth rate can still shrink if deaths and emigration are higher. To judge the change in size, work out additions minus removals over a stated time.\n\nWorked example. A population of 200 antelope in a reserve has 40 births, 15 deaths, 5 immigrants and 10 emigrants in a year. Additions are 40 + 5 = 45 and removals are 15 + 10 = 25, so the population grows by 20 to 220.',loc='45.3 Environmental Limits to Population Growth')
C('5.3-02','FEATURE','State one factor that increases the size of a population.','Births (reproduction) or immigration.',['1 mark: names one factor that adds individuals, births or immigration.'])
D('5.3-03','Community','A community is all of the populations of different species in an ecosystem.',
 'It includes the living populations only. The non-living water, soil, light and temperature are part of the wider ecosystem.',
 'The fish, algae, insects and microorganisms in a pond form a community of interacting populations. A list of fish alone is not the whole community. A water temperature reading is a physical factor of the environment and not a population. A community study must therefore include several groups of organisms and the links between them.',['a community is made up of all the populations in an area','the populations are of different species living in one ecosystem'])
D('5.3-04','Ecosystem','An ecosystem is a unit containing the community of organisms and their environment, interacting together.',
 'A decomposing log or a pond can be studied as an ecosystem, because organisms interact with each other and with physical and chemical conditions.',
 'Light, temperature, dissolved gases and nutrients affect the organisms. The organisms in turn change the shade, the oxygen and the nutrient supply. An ecosystem is therefore more than a place name or a list of animals. The Namib Desert is an ecosystem. Its organisms, such as Welwitschia plants and fog-basking beetles, are adapted to the dry conditions and the fog that drifts in from the Atlantic Ocean.',['an ecosystem contains a community of organisms and their environment','the community and the environment interact as a unit'])
E('5.3-05','Sigmoid population growth','Where resources are limited, a population grows through a lag phase, an exponential (log) phase and a stationary phase, and may then enter a death phase.',
 'As the population becomes denser, shortage of resources or other pressures reduce net growth. The carrying capacity depends on conditions and is not a permanent fixed number.',
 'The curve is S-shaped, which is the meaning of sigmoid. In the lag phase there are few breeding individuals and they are still settling in and maturing, so growth is slow. In the exponential (log) phase resources are plentiful and limiting factors are weak, so births greatly exceed deaths and numbers rise steeply. In the stationary phase food, water, space, disease or predation limit growth, births roughly equal deaths, and the curve levels off. If a resource runs out or waste builds up, deaths exceed births and the population falls: this is the death phase.\n\nA level curve means that net change is close to zero, because additions are roughly equal to removals. It does not mean that births and deaths have stopped. A steep curve means a large increase per unit time. A high but flat curve means a large population with little change.',loc='45.3 Environmental Limits to Population Growth')
C('5.3-05','APP','Fig. VIS-6116-5.3-05 is a graph of population size against time for a population with limited resources. The curve is S-shaped, rising steeply in the middle and then levelling off at a high population size. Explain the difference between the steep region and the level region.','In the steep region births are much greater than deaths, so the population grows quickly while resources are plentiful. In the level region limiting factors such as food or space mean that additions and removals are about equal, so the population size stays roughly constant.',['1 mark: in the steep region of this curve, births greatly exceed deaths while resources are plentiful, so numbers increase quickly.','1 mark: in the level region, limiting factors such as food or space make additions and removals about equal, so the size stays roughly constant.'],True)
E('5.3-06','Human population increase','Where births exceed deaths and movement of people does not offset the difference, the human population increases.',
 'When limiting factors are weak and the growth rate stays constant, the number added each year gets bigger, because the same percentage is applied to a larger population.',
 'Worked example. A population of 1000 grows by 2% each year with no migration. The first year adds 20 people to give 1020. The next year adds 2% of 1020, which is about 20.4. The additions get larger every year, although the percentage rate is the same.\n\nHuman numbers have risen quickly because better medical care, sanitation and food supply have reduced death rates. Birth patterns also change over time. This is a model of how growth works and not a forecast.',loc='45.5 Human Population Growth')
C('5.3-06','PROC','Describe how a human population can increase when limiting factors are weak and births exceed deaths.','More people are born than die, so there are net additions. As the population becomes larger, the same percentage growth then adds more people each interval, so the increase accelerates.',['1 mark: births exceed deaths, so there are net additions each year.','1 mark: the same percentage growth applied to a larger population then adds more people each interval, so growth accelerates.'])
E('5.3-07','Population size and environmental demand','Better survival can increase the size of a population when other rates do not offset it. A larger population raises the demand for food, water, housing and energy.',
 'The effect on the environment also depends on how much each person consumes, on technology and on management, so the number of people alone does not decide the impact.',
 'More people need more farmland for food, and farming uses land and water. More people produce more domestic waste, which needs collection and treatment. More housing can replace natural habitat, and more water use can lower river flows. Good planning, efficient use of resources and fair access can reduce these pressures.',loc='45.5 Human Population Growth')
C('5.3-07','FEATURE','Describe two environmental demands that can rise as a human population increases, if consumption per person stays similar.','Food production needs more land and water. More domestic waste is produced and needs collection and treatment. More housing, water and energy are also needed.',['Credit any two distinct points, 1 mark each, such as: more land and water for growing food; more domestic waste needing collection and treatment; more housing, which replaces natural habitat; more water use, which lowers river flows or groundwater; more energy or fuel use; more trees cut for firewood or timber.'])
C('5.3-07','WHY','Explain why population size alone cannot decide how much environmental impact an area has.','The impact also depends on how much each person uses and on technology and management. Two areas with equal populations can have different impacts if one uses resources more efficiently and treats waste.',['1 mark: impact also depends on how much each person consumes, because the same number of people can use very different amounts of resources.','1 mark: technology and management, such as waste treatment or efficient resource use, change the damage done, so equal populations can have different impacts.'])
E('5.3-08','Reading population graphs','To read a population graph, check the time scale, the units and whether it shows total size, yearly additions or a percentage rate.',
 'A steeper size curve means a greater increase per unit time. A rising total can occur while the percentage growth rate is falling.',
 'Worked example. A model town has 20 000 people. The growth rate is 5.0% in the first year, 4.0% in the second year and 2.5% in the third year.\n\nYear 1: 5.0% of 20 000 adds 1000 people, giving 21 000.\nYear 2: 4.0% of 21 000 adds 840 people, giving 21 840.\nYear 3: 2.5% of 21 840 adds 546 people, giving 22 386.\n\nThe percentage rate has halved but the population has kept rising, because a smaller percentage of a larger population still adds people. Always check whether a graph shows size, additions or rate before describing the trend.')
C('5.3-08','APP','Fig. VIS-6116-5.3-08 is a graph of the data in the table, for a model town. The total population rises each year while the percentage growth rate falls. Suggest why the total population keeps rising while the percentage growth rate falls.\n\n| Year | Population | Growth rate in the year (%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 20 000 | not applicable |\n| 1 | 21 000 | 5.0 |\n| 2 | 21 840 | 4.0 |\n| 3 | 22 386 | 2.5 |','The growth rate falls from 5.0% to 2.5%, but the population rises each year from 20 000 to 22 386, adding 1000, 840 and 546 people. A smaller percentage of a larger population still adds people, so the total keeps rising, but more slowly.',['1 mark: uses the given table to show that each year still adds people, 1000, 840 and 546, although the additions get smaller.','1 mark: because the percentage is applied to a larger population, a smaller rate still gives a positive increase, so the total keeps rising.'],True)

# ---------------- 5.4.1 Human influences on ecosystems ----------------
E('5.4.1-01','Monocultures','A large monoculture replaces a more varied habitat with a single crop, reducing the range of food and shelter available to other organisms.',
 'A uniform crop can allow pests and disease to spread quickly, and repeated cultivation of one crop can use up particular soil nutrients and increase the need for fertiliser and pesticides.',
 'A field of one crop is not completely without other species, but it supports far fewer than the natural vegetation it replaced. Fewer plant species mean fewer insects, birds and soil organisms.\n\nPests spread easily because their food is spread across the whole field with no gaps. Farmers then often use more pesticide, which can harm other organisms. A single crop also takes the same nutrients from the soil each year.\n\nHigh production can help to meet food needs, so the ecosystem cost depends on how the field is managed. Rotating crops, leaving strips of natural vegetation and protecting the soil can reduce some of the effects.',loc='47.3 Threats to Biodiversity')
C('5.4.1-01','FEATURE','Describe two possible negative effects of a large-scale monoculture on the ecosystem.','Habitat is simplified, so biodiversity falls because fewer species have food and shelter. A continuous crop of one species lets pests and disease spread quickly. The same soil nutrients are also used up each year.',['Credit any two distinct points, 1 mark each, such as: fewer species, because food and shelter are reduced; pests or disease spreading quickly through a continuous crop; the same soil nutrients used up each year, so more fertiliser is needed; more pesticide used, which can harm other organisms.'])
E('5.4.1-02','Intensive livestock production','Large numbers of livestock kept close together produce concentrated manure and waste, which can pollute water if it is not stored and treated properly.',
 'Growing feed and keeping animals demands land and water, and too many animals on the land can damage vegetation and soil.',
 'Intensive housing and overgrazing are different problems. In a feedlot or a large poultry house, waste is concentrated in one place, and it can run into streams and cause eutrophication. Overgrazing happens on open land when too many animals eat the plant cover, which leaves bare soil open to erosion. In Namibia, where large areas are used as rangeland for cattle, goats and sheep, careful stocking rates help to protect the vegetation.\n\nManure is a useful fertiliser when it is applied in suitable amounts. It becomes a pollutant when more is released than the soil and crops can use.',loc='47.3 Threats to Biodiversity')
C('5.4.1-02','FEATURE','Describe two environmental risks of poorly managed intensive livestock production.','Concentrated manure can pollute water with nutrients and organic matter. Growing feed needs a lot of land and water, which can damage other habitats. Too many animals on open land can also cause overgrazing and soil erosion.',['Credit any two distinct points, 1 mark each, such as: manure or waste washing into water, causing eutrophication; overgrazing that removes plant cover and leads to soil erosion; land cleared to grow feed, which removes other habitats; heavy use of water.'])
E('5.4.1-03','Providing sufficient food','Increasing the food supply can improve nutrition and livelihoods but may need more land, water, energy and money.',
 'Distribution, affordability and waste reduction affect food security as well as total production, and environmental damage can undermine future food supply.',
 'A social effect concerns people\'s health and access to food. An economic effect concerns income and costs. An environmental effect concerns habitats and resources. These effects are linked: damaged soil lowers future yields, which lowers farmers\' income.\n\nWhen you discuss food supply, explain the link in each case and do not just list three words. Producing more food is one answer to shortage. Reducing food losses in storage and transport, and making existing food accessible, can help without clearing more land.')
C('5.4.1-03','CHAIN','Discuss one social, one economic and one environmental consequence of increasing food provision.','More accessible food can reduce undernutrition. Investment can create income but raises production costs. Expanding farmland can remove habitats, which reduces biodiversity.',['1 mark: a social effect, such as less undernutrition because more food is available to people.','1 mark: an economic consequence, such as more income for farmers, or higher costs because seed, fertiliser or irrigation must be paid for.','1 mark: an environmental consequence, such as habitat cleared for farmland, so biodiversity decreases, or more fertiliser running off into water.'])
C('5.4.1-03','APP','A district grows enough grain overall, but 30% spoils in storage and remote households cannot reach the markets. Suggest why improving storage and transport may help more than clearing more habitat for crops.','The district already grows enough, so the shortage comes from spoilage and poor access, not from too small a harvest. Better storage and transport make the existing food available and avoid removing more habitat.',['1 mark: the district already grows enough grain, so the shortage here comes from spoilage and poor access, not from too small a harvest.','1 mark: more crops would still spoil or fail to reach remote households, so better storage and transport help more and no habitat is cleared.'])
E('5.4.1-04','Reasons for habitat destruction','Land can be cleared for crops, livestock, housing, roads, mining and other development, removing or fragmenting habitat.',
 'Demand for timber or fuel can also drive clearance. The reason for clearing is different from its ecological consequence.',
 'A road can divide a connected habitat even when most of the land remains, which affects movement and breeding. When you answer a question about causes, name the activity or the demand behind it. When you answer a question about effects, explain what happens to the organisms, soil or water afterwards.',loc='47.3 Threats to Biodiversity')
C('5.4.1-04','FEATURE','State one human activity that can cause habitat destruction.','Clearing natural vegetation to make crop fields.',['1 mark: names one activity that removes habitat, such as clearing land for crops or grazing, building houses or roads, mining or logging.'])
E('5.4.1-05','Effects of deforestation','Removing forest destroys habitat. Without roots to bind the soil and a canopy to break the force of rain, the soil is more easily eroded.',
 'Deforestation also changes how much water the land absorbs and releases, and it reduces carbon storage. The effects on local water flow and climate depend on the site.',
 'Follow the mechanism step by step. Exposed soil is hit directly by raindrops and is washed into streams, where the sediment can harm aquatic life. With less water soaking in, more runs off quickly, which can make flooding worse in some catchments. The carbon cycle effects of deforestation are explained in section 5.2.',loc='47.3 Threats to Biodiversity')
C('5.4.1-05','CHAIN','Explain how deforestation can increase soil erosion during heavy rain.','Removing the canopy and roots exposes the soil and reduces its binding. Raindrop impact and runoff then loosen and carry away more soil.',['1 mark: when trees are removed, there are no roots to bind the soil and no canopy to break the force of the rain.','1 mark: raindrops then loosen the bare soil and runoff carries it away, so the effect is more erosion.'])
C('5.4.1-05','APP','Two slopes receive equal heavy rain. One has been cleared and the other keeps its roots and ground cover. Explain why the stream below the cleared slope may become muddier.','The cleared slope has no ground cover to break the force of the rain and no roots to bind the soil. More soil is loosened and washed into the stream, so it carries more sediment even though the rainfall is the same.',['1 mark: on this cleared slope there are no roots to bind the soil and no cover to shield it, so raindrops loosen it.','1 mark: more runoff carries the loosened soil into the stream, so it carries more sediment even though the rainfall was the same.'])
famine=[
 ('06','Unequal distribution of food','Food can exist in sufficient total amounts while some groups cannot obtain it, because transport, markets or sharing are unequal.','A shortage in one place can occur without a shortage in the world as a whole.','Availability somewhere is different from access where it is needed. Blocked roads, conflict and exclusion can stop food reaching people even after a good harvest. The right response depends on the barrier: another harvest does not reopen a destroyed road.\n\nFamine is a severe shortage of food that affects a large number of people and leads to starvation and death. Its causes often combine.',
  'Explain how unequal distribution of food can contribute to famine.','Food is available elsewhere, but the affected people cannot get enough of it. A long period without enough food causes severe undernutrition even though the wider total is enough.',['1 mark: food does not reach some groups, because of poor transport, markets or unequal sharing, even though enough exists elsewhere.','1 mark: these people then get less food than they need for a long time, so the effect is severe undernutrition and starvation.'],
  'A region has full grain stores, but the bridges to three villages are destroyed. Explain why a food surplus for the region does not rule out severe shortage in those villages.','The destroyed bridges stop the grain reaching the villages. The regional surplus is not the same as the food that the villagers can actually obtain.',['1 mark: with the bridges gone, the grain in this region\'s stores cannot be transported to the villages.','1 mark: famine depends on the food people can actually obtain, so a regional surplus does not prevent shortage in the villages.']),
 ('07','Drought','Drought reduces the water available for crops and livestock and can reduce harvests and animal production.','It can also lower income and raise food prices, which reduces access as well as supply.','Namibia has a dry climate and highly variable rainfall, so drought is a recurring risk for crop and livestock farmers. The effect depends on stored food, irrigation, drought-tolerant crop varieties and support. A rainfall shortage does not always cause famine where food stores and support are available.\n\nExplain the whole chain: water shortage leads to lower production, which leads to less food and less income, which leads to less access to food.',
  'Explain how drought can contribute to famine.','Water shortage reduces crop and livestock production. Less food, and less income from farming, mean people cannot obtain enough to eat.',['1 mark: less water means crop yields and livestock production decrease.','1 mark: there is then less food to eat, or less farm income to buy it, so the effect is that people cannot get enough food.'],
  'Two drought-affected districts lose equal amounts of harvest, but one has food reserves and funded distribution. Explain why equal crop loss need not lead to equal famine risk.','The reserves and distribution replace the lost local supply in one district. This protection breaks the link between crop loss and food shortage there.',['1 mark: in the district with reserves, stored food and funded distribution replace this lost harvest.','1 mark: people there can still obtain enough food while the other district cannot, because famine depends on access to food, not only on the harvest.']),
 ('08','Flooding','Floods can destroy crops, kill livestock, damage food stores and cut transport, which reduces both food supply and access.','Waterlogging cuts off the oxygen that roots need and can reduce crop growth even after the water has gone.','Floodwater can also contaminate drinking water and spread disease, which adds to the harm from poor nutrition. In northern Namibia, seasonal floods from the Cuvelai system can cover large areas and cut off communities. A small seasonal flood can have a very different effect from a severe one, so timing, size and preparation matter.\n\nWater is not always good for plants. Too much water can be as harmful as too little when roots cannot get oxygen.',
  'Explain how severe flooding can contribute to famine.','Floods can destroy harvests and stores and block roads. Less food is usable and less food is delivered, so households cannot get enough food.',['1 mark: floods destroy crops and food stores, or kill livestock, so less food is available.','1 mark: floods also block roads, which stops food being delivered, with the effect that households cannot get enough to eat.'],
  'After a flood, undamaged grain is stored in a town 60 km away. Households had about one week of food at home, and the only road to the town has been closed for five weeks. Explain why households may still face food shortage even though no grain was damaged.','The closed road stops the grain being delivered, so food is available but households cannot reach it. Their food at home lasted only about a week, so it ran out long before the road reopened.',['1 mark: this closed road stops the stored grain reaching households, so food is available but not accessible.','1 mark: the food households had at home lasted only about a week, so it runs out while the road stays closed and they face shortage although the grain is safe.']),
 ('09','Population increase and food supply','If the population grows faster than the food supply that people can reach, the food available per person falls.','Population growth alone does not guarantee famine, because production, trade, storage and access can also change.','Food per person is the food supply divided by the number of people. An unchanged harvest shared among twice as many people gives half as much each. A harvest that grows as fast as the population keeps the amount per person the same.\n\nWorked example. If food rises by 10% and population rises by 25%, food per person changes by 1.10 ÷ 1.25 = 0.88, which is 12% lower than before.',
  'Explain how population increase can contribute to famine if the accessible food supply does not increase.','More people share the same food. The food available per person falls and may become too little to meet nutritional needs.',['1 mark: when the population increases but the food supply does not, the same food is shared among more people.','1 mark: food per person decreases, so the effect is that people may get too little to meet their needs.'],
  'The accessible food in a model district rises by 10% while its population rises by 25%. Explain why more total food does not mean more food per person.','The population rises faster than the food total. Dividing the new supply by the larger population gives 1.10 ÷ 1.25 = 0.88, so each person has about 12% less than before.',['1 mark: calculates food per person from the given changes, 1.10 ÷ 1.25 = 0.88, about 12% less than before.','1 mark: because the population has grown faster than the food supply, each person\'s share falls even though total food has risen.']),
 ('10','Poverty and access to food','Poverty can stop households buying food even when markets have enough in stock.','Poverty also limits access to land, irrigation, storage and farm inputs, which makes households more vulnerable to harvest loss.','Affordability and availability are different problems. Shops with full shelves do not help a household that cannot afford to buy. Rising prices or lost wages can reduce access without changing the size of the harvest.\n\nLong-term responses include secure jobs and income, and support for the poorest households, as well as growing more food.',
  'Explain how poverty can contribute to famine even when there is food in the markets.','Households may not have enough money to buy the food. If this continues, people become severely undernourished even though stocks exist.',['1 mark: when households have too little money, they cannot buy the food that is in the markets.','1 mark: if this continues, they eat less than they need for a long period, so the effect is severe undernutrition.'],
  'The grain stocks in a market stay constant while the price of grain doubles. A household\'s income does not change. Explain why the household\'s food security may worsen even though the harvest has not fallen.','The same income buys half as much grain at double the price. The price rise reduces the household\'s access to food even though total production is unchanged.',['1 mark: with the price doubled and income unchanged, this household can buy only about half as much grain.','1 mark: food security depends on being able to afford food, so access falls even though the harvest and stocks have not changed.'])]
for n,h,c1,c2,more,p,a,g,ap,aa,ag in famine:
 o='5.4.1-'+n;E(o,h,c1,c2,more)
 C(o,'CHAIN',p,a,g)
 C(o,'APP',ap,aa,ag)
pollutants=[
 ('11','Chemical waste','Industrial discharges and farm chemicals can enter rivers, lakes and coastal water through drains and runoff.','Toxic substances can damage organisms and disrupt food webs. The effects depend on the substance, the concentration and the length of exposure.','Not all chemical pollution is nutrient enrichment. A pesticide can poison organisms that were not its target, such as fish and aquatic insects, and a herbicide can kill water plants. Some substances persist for a long time and are carried through food chains.\n\nThe cause of damage should be matched to the evidence. If a chemical kills algae immediately and no nutrients were added, direct toxicity is the explanation, not an algal bloom.','Pesticide sprayed on a field is washed into a river by rain. State one harmful effect of the pesticide on the river.','It can poison non-target organisms such as fish and aquatic insects.','1 mark for one correct point, such as the pesticide poisoning fish, aquatic insects or other organisms that were not its target.'),
 ('12','Discarded rubbish','Rubbish can reach water through dumping, drains and wind.','Animals can become tangled in it or swallow it, and piles of waste can damage habitats and make water unusable.','Physical harm and poisoning are different. A loop of fishing line can trap an animal without any chemical being involved. A blocked drain can raise the risk of flooding. In the sea, turtles, seals and seabirds can become entangled in discarded fishing line and nets or swallow plastic pieces.\n\nPreventing rubbish at source, collecting it and recycling it each deal with a different part of the problem.','Plastic rubbish is dumped near a river. State one harmful effect of the rubbish on animals living in the river.','Animals can become entangled in it or swallow it.','1 mark for one correct point: animals become entangled in the rubbish, or swallow it, or lose habitat under it.'),
 ('13','Untreated sewage','Untreated sewage puts organic matter, disease-causing microorganisms and nutrients into water.','Microorganisms that break down the organic matter use up dissolved oxygen, and the pathogens are a health risk.','The loss of oxygen starts directly with the breakdown of organic waste. It does not need an algal bloom first. The nutrients in sewage can also cause excess growth of algae and plants.\n\nWater that looks clear can still carry pathogens, so drinking water must be treated and tested.','Untreated sewage is released into a river. State one harmful effect on the river or on people.','The decomposers breaking it down use up dissolved oxygen, or pathogens in it cause disease.','1 mark for one correct point, such as decomposers using up dissolved oxygen so fish die, nutrients causing algal growth, or pathogens causing disease in people.'),
 ('14','Fertilisers as pollutants','Rain and drainage can carry excess nitrate and phosphate from fertiliser from fields into water.','The added nutrients cause rapid growth of algae and plants, which can lead to eutrophication.','Fertiliser helps a crop when the amount and timing suit its needs. Nutrients that the crop does not take up are washed off the field or leached through the soil. Nitrate is very soluble, so it is easily carried into streams and groundwater.\n\nThe full sequence of eutrophication is described in the next section. Here the important point is how fertiliser reaches the water.','Fertiliser is washed from a field into a lake. State one harmful effect on the lake.','Excess nutrients cause rapid growth of algae (an algal bloom), which leads to eutrophication.','1 mark for one correct point, such as an algal bloom, eutrophication, or loss of oxygen leading to fish deaths.')]
for n,h,c1,c2,more,p,a,g in pollutants:
 o='5.4.1-'+n;E(o,h,c1,c2,more);C(o,'FEATURE',p,a,[g])
E('5.4.1-15','Eutrophication','Excess nutrients cause rapid growth of algae and plants. Dense growth shades submerged plants, and when the algae and plants die the dead organic material is broken down by aerobic microorganisms, which use up the oxygen in the water.',
 'The low oxygen concentration kills fish and other sensitive animals. Sewage adds organic matter that also uses oxygen, and pesticides can kill organisms by poisoning, not by enrichment.',
 'Use the sequence to place observations. A lake receives fertiliser runoff in summer. Within two weeks the surface turns green; a month later the dissolved oxygen near the bottom has fallen from 8 to 2 mg per dm³ and dead fish are found. The green surface is the algal bloom, the low oxygen is the result of decomposers respiring on the dead material, and the dead fish are the final effect. The fish did not die of the nutrients themselves: the missing step is the oxygen loss.\n\nKeep the causes separate. Sewage can lower oxygen directly, because its organic matter is broken down by microorganisms, and it adds nutrients too. A pesticide kills by poison, so it does not start the same sequence.\n\nAlgae produce oxygen in daylight, but they also respire, and the great quantity of dead material at the end of a bloom is the reason oxygen falls so far.',loc='46.3 Biogeochemical Cycles')
C('5.4.1-15','PROC','Describe how excess nutrient input can lead to low oxygen and fish deaths in a lake.','Excess nutrients cause rapid growth of algae and plants, which later die and leave a lot of dead material. Decomposers then respire aerobically on it and use up the oxygen, so fish die.',['1 mark: excess nutrients cause rapid growth of algae and plants, which then die, adding a lot of dead material.','1 mark: decomposers respire aerobically on the dead material and use up the oxygen, so fish die.'])
C('5.4.1-15','MECH','Describe the complete eutrophication process from nutrient input to harm to fish.','Excess nutrients stimulate an algal bloom. The bloom shades plants below, and the algae and plants die, increasing the dead organic matter. Decomposers respire aerobically on this material and use up the dissolved oxygen. The oxygen falls below what fish need, so fish are stressed or die.',['1 mark: excess nitrate or phosphate causes algae to grow rapidly, forming a bloom.','1 mark: the bloom shades plants below, and the algae and plants die, so dead organic matter increases.','1 mark: decomposers respire aerobically on the dead matter, which uses up the dissolved oxygen.','1 mark: oxygen falls below the level fish need, with the effect that fish die.'])
E('5.4.1-16','Recycling and waste','Recycling recovers useful material from waste, which reduces the demand for new raw materials and cuts the amount of litter and waste that has to be disposed of.',
 'The benefits depend on safe collection, sorting and processing. Hazardous waste needs proper treatment.',
 'Recycling does not remove the need to reduce unnecessary use. A collection point must lead to a real recovery route. Moving waste from a street to an open dump only moves the problem, because wind and rain can carry it back into rivers and drains.\n\nGlass, metal, paper and many plastics can be collected and reprocessed into new products. Recycling saves some of the energy and raw material that would be needed to make the product from scratch. A cleaner environment also lowers the risk of injury and blocked drains.')
C('5.4.1-16','APP','Collected plastic bottles are piled on open ground beside a river and left there. Suggest why this is not recycling and may still pollute the river.','Recycling means the bottles are processed into new material, but here they are only stored. Rain and wind can wash the bottles back into the river, so the pollution risk is still there.',['1 mark: recycling means processing the bottles into new material, which has not happened here, so the bottles are only stored.','1 mark: rain and wind can carry the bottles into the river, so the pollution risk is still there.'])
E('5.4.1-17','Fertiliser overuse','Using more fertiliser than the crop needs leaves soluble nutrients in the soil that are washed out into water, and very high concentrations can harm plants.',
 'Extra fertiliser wastes money and resources and increases pollution without a matching increase in yield.',
 'Match the amount and timing to the soil and crop. Heavy rain soon after fertiliser is spread carries nutrients off the field, especially from bare soil. Extra nitrate does not help if another factor, such as water, is limiting growth.\n\nHigh concentrations of fertiliser in the soil can also damage plant roots, because water tends to leave the roots when the soil solution is too concentrated. A soil test shows what nutrients are really needed, so the farmer can apply the correct amount.')
C('5.4.1-17','FEATURE','Describe two possible dangers of applying too much fertiliser.','Nutrients washed into water can cause eutrophication. Nitrate can leach into groundwater used for drinking. Very high concentrations can damage plant roots, and the extra fertiliser wastes money.',['Credit any two distinct points, 1 mark each, such as: nutrients washed into rivers or lakes, causing eutrophication; nitrate leaching into groundwater used for drinking; very concentrated fertiliser damaging plant roots; money and resources wasted with no extra yield.'])
E('5.4.1-18','Alternatives to industrial fertiliser','Compost and manure return organic material and nutrients to the soil, and rotating crops with legumes can add nitrogen through nitrogen fixation.',
 'Soil testing, accurate application and leaving crop residues in the field can reduce the need for manufactured fertiliser. Organic sources can also pollute water if too much is applied.',
 'A legume crop such as cowpea helps the next crop if its residues stay in the field and decompose, which releases the nitrogen it fixed. If the whole plant is removed, much of the nitrogen leaves with it. In northern Namibia, cowpeas can be grown alongside or in rotation with mahangu, the pearl millet that is the staple grain.\n\nCompost improves soil structure, but it releases its nutrients more slowly than a mineral fertiliser. A natural source is not automatically safe or sufficient. Manure can still run off and cause eutrophication if too much is spread.')
C('5.4.1-18','CHAIN','Discuss three ways to reduce reliance on large amounts of industrial fertiliser, and their consequences.','Compost and manure recycle nutrients and organic matter but must be applied in controlled amounts. Legume crops in rotation add fixed nitrogen when their residues return it to the soil. Soil testing matches the fertiliser to crop need, which reduces excess and runoff.',['1 mark each for any three ways, each with its consequence.','Creditable ways include: compost or manure recycles nutrients and organic matter, so less manufactured fertiliser is needed, although too much can still run off; legumes in rotation add fixed nitrogen when their residues decompose, which increases soil nitrogen for the next crop; soil testing matches the amount to crop need, so there is less excess and less runoff; leaving crop residues in the field returns nutrients, with the effect that less fertiliser is bought.'])
C('5.4.1-18','APP','A farmer grows a legume crop and then removes all the above-ground material from the field at harvest. Suggest why the next crop will not receive all the nitrogen that the legume fixed.','Much of the fixed nitrogen is in the plant tissue that was removed. Most of the benefit to the next crop comes from material that stays in the field and decomposes.',['1 mark: most of the fixed nitrogen is in the plant tissue, and in this harvest that tissue has been removed from the field.','1 mark: because little residue is left to decompose, less nitrogen is released into the soil, so the next crop receives less.'])
E('5.4.1-19','Causes of acid rain','Sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides, from sources such as burning fossil fuels, react in the air to form acids that fall in rain.',
 'Wind can carry the pollutants far from their source before they fall, so the effects need not be beside the place that released them.',
 'Burning coal and oil that contain sulfur releases sulfur dioxide. High-temperature combustion in engines forms nitrogen oxides from the nitrogen and oxygen of the air. Both gases dissolve in water droplets to form acids.\n\nAcid rain is a different problem from the enhanced greenhouse effect. Both involve burning fuel, but acid rain is caused by sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides, and global warming is caused mainly by carbon dioxide and methane. Natural rain is slightly acidic. The pollution problem is the extra acidity.')
C('5.4.1-19','FEATURE','State one gas released by burning fossil fuels that can cause acid rain.','Sulfur dioxide (or nitrogen oxides).',['1 mark: names one correct gas, sulfur dioxide or nitrogen oxides (not carbon dioxide alone).'])
E('5.4.1-20','Effects of acid rain','Acid rain lowers the pH of lakes and soil, which can harm aquatic organisms and release harmful metal ions from the soil.',
 'It can also damage leaves and trees and attack limestone and some metals in buildings. The effect depends on how well the water or soil can neutralise acid and how much acid falls.',
 'A lake in an area with limestone can neutralise acid better than a lake in an area of hard rock, so the same acid rain can have different effects in different places. A fall in pH means the water is more acidic. Fish eggs and young animals are often the first to be harmed.\n\nWhen you describe an effect, say exactly what happens, for example that acidified water kills fish or that acid damages tree leaves. Do not just write that acid rain is poisonous.')
C('5.4.1-20','FEATURE','State one environmental effect of acid rain.','Acidification of lakes can kill fish and other aquatic organisms.',['1 mark: names one correct effect, such as harm to fish and other aquatic organisms, damage to tree leaves, or damage to limestone buildings.'])
E('5.4.1-21','Reducing acid rain','Reducing the release of sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides reduces the amount of acid that forms in the air.',
 'Using fuels with less sulfur, treating exhaust gases and using cleaner energy or transport all deal with the source. Treating a lake does not stop the emissions.',
 'Match the action to the gas. Flue-gas treatment can remove sulfur dioxide from the exhaust of a power station or smelter, and catalytic converters in vehicle exhausts reduce nitrogen oxides. Using less fuel, through efficiency or less travel, reduces both gases.\n\nPreventing the emissions is different from repairing damage afterwards. Adding lime to an acidified lake can raise its pH for a time, but the acid will return if the emissions continue.')
C('5.4.1-21','FEATURE','State one way to reduce the release of the gases that cause acid rain.','Remove sulfur dioxide from industrial exhaust gases before they leave the chimney.',['1 mark: names one correct measure that reduces emissions, such as treating exhaust gases, using low-sulfur fuel, using catalytic converters or burning less fossil fuel.'])
E('5.4.1-22','Persistent plastics','Plastics that are not biodegradable stay for a very long time on land and in water and cause entanglement, swallowing of plastic and damage to habitats.',
 'When plastic breaks into smaller pieces it has not been broken down by living things. Small particles remain and can be carried widely.',
 'An animal that swallows plastic may have its gut blocked or may feel full and feed less. Net and line fragments can trap limbs. Plastic pieces in the sea can be swallowed by seabirds, turtles and fish.\n\nPrevention works at different stages: using less plastic, reusing it, collecting and recycling it, and stopping litter at source. Burning plastic without proper controls produces harmful gases, so it does not solve the problem.')
C('5.4.1-22','CHAIN','Discuss three effects of non-biodegradable plastic waste on ecosystems.','Plastic does not break down, so it builds up on land and in water. Animals can become entangled in it or swallow it. When it fragments it spreads as smaller particles, so the exposure continues.',['1 mark: non-biodegradable plastic is not broken down by decomposers, so it builds up on land and in water.','1 mark: animals become entangled in it or swallow it, which can block the gut or cause injury and death.','1 mark: when it breaks into smaller pieces, the fragments spread widely, so the effect lasts a long time.'])
C('5.4.1-22','APP','During a litter survey, a plastic bag breaks into small pieces, but the total mass of plastic is unchanged. Suggest why a fall in the number of whole bags does not show that the plastic has biodegraded.','The change observed is fragmentation, not breakdown by living organisms. The total mass is unchanged, so the pieces remain, and counting whole bags gives a false picture.',['1 mark: this bag has only broken into smaller pieces, which is fragmentation, not breakdown by living organisms.','1 mark: because the total mass of plastic is unchanged, all the plastic is still there, so fewer whole bags does not show biodegradation.'])
A.notes['unresolved_mapping']={'pesticide_uptake':'No inference of diffusion or active transport is made from the disputed historical evidence; mapping remains unresolved in the approved planning record.'}

# ---------------- 5.4.2 Conservation ----------------
D('5.4.2-01','Conservation','Conservation is maintaining the environment and natural resources in a way that maintains biodiversity.',
 'It includes managing the use of resources and protecting ecological processes. It is more than keeping a few attractive animals alive.',
 'Biodiversity includes the number of species and the variation within species, and habitat condition supports their survival. Conservation can involve restoring damaged habitat, monitoring populations and agreeing sustainable use of a resource.\n\nA few animals in an enclosure are not enough to show that a species or an ecosystem is secure. Conservation has to protect the habitat and the population in the wild, as well as the individual animals.',['conservation maintains the environment and natural resources','this is done in a way that maintains biodiversity'],head='What conservation means')
E('5.4.2-02','Conserving fossil fuels','Fossil fuels are non-renewable on a human timescale because they form far more slowly than they are used.',
 'Using them efficiently reduces depletion and the environmental effects of extracting and burning them, and it keeps options open while alternatives are developed.',
 'The stock of fossil fuels is finite even if its exact size is not known. Efficiency means getting the same service from less fuel, for example a car with a more efficient engine. Reducing unnecessary use means needing less of the service in the first place. Both lower the rate of use, but neither makes a fuel renewable.\n\nA new oil field does not change the fact that oil forms over millions of years. When you judge a rate of use, compare it with the time the resource takes to form, and think about the carbon dioxide released when it is burnt.')
C('5.4.2-02','CHAIN','Explain why fossil fuels need conservation even though new deposits are sometimes discovered.','Finding a new deposit does not make a fuel form quickly. Continued use draws down a finite store, so using less fuel slows depletion and reduces its environmental effects.',['1 mark: fossil fuels form over millions of years, much more slowly than they are used, so new discoveries do not renew them.','1 mark: because the total store is finite, using less fuel slows depletion, with the effect that the resource lasts longer and less carbon dioxide is released.'])
D('5.4.2-03','Sustainable development','Sustainable development is development that provides for the needs of an increasing human population while preserving the environment.',
 'The aim is to link present wellbeing with keeping the resources and ecological processes that are needed in future.',
 'A project may raise output now but damage the soil or water that people will need later. That would not be sustainable, however much income it brings in the short term. To assess a project, look at the balance of resources, the pollution and who has access, as well as the immediate income.\n\nPreserving the environment does not mean that nobody may use it. Use must be managed so that the supporting ecosystem stays healthy. For example, a forest can supply timber for many years if trees are felled no faster than new ones grow.',['sustainable development provides for the needs of an increasing human population','it preserves the environment at the same time'])
E('5.4.2-04','Sustaining forests and fish stocks','Education can improve the way people use resources, quotas can limit how much is taken, and restocking can help depleted populations to recover.',
 'These measures need monitoring, suitable habitat and effective management. Adding young organisms cannot make up for taking far too many.',
 'A quota is a limit on how much may be taken, for example the mass of fish landed in a year. A quota helps only if it is set at a level the stock can sustain, and if it is enforced. Namibia manages its hake fishery in the Benguela ecosystem with annual catch quotas.\n\nRestocking, for example replanting trees or releasing young fish, works only if the species, the habitat and the genetic variety are suitable. Forests need time and protection to regrow. Fish stocks need enough breeding adults and nursery areas such as shallow, sheltered water.',loc='47.4 Preserving Biodiversity')
C('5.4.2-04','FEATURE','State one way a monitored quota can help to sustain a fish stock.','It limits the number of fish removed so that enough breeding fish remain to replace those caught.',['1 mark: names one correct point, that the quota limits the catch so enough fish are left to breed and replenish the stock.'])
E('5.4.2-05','Treating sewage','Sewage treatment removes large solids and suspended material and then uses controlled biological treatment to reduce organic pollution and oxygen demand.',
 'The treated water is tested and may be disinfected before it is released or reused. Treated effluent is not automatically fit to drink.',
 'The stages in a typical works are as follows. Screens remove large objects such as rags and plastic. Settling tanks let the solid particles sink as sludge. In the aeration tanks, air is bubbled through the liquid so that aerobic bacteria break down the dissolved organic matter. The liquid is then settled again to separate the bacterial sludge, and the cleaned water is tested and released or reused. The sludge is usually treated in separate tanks and may be used as fertiliser if it is safe.\n\nAppearance cannot show whether water is safe. Pathogens, nutrients or dissolved chemicals can remain if the treatment is not complete.')
C('5.4.2-05','PROC','Outline three main stages that reduce the pollution from sewage before the water is released.','Screens remove large objects such as rags and plastic. Settling tanks then let solid particles sink as sludge. Aerobic bacteria in aerated tanks then break down the dissolved organic matter, so the released water uses up much less oxygen in the river.',['1 mark: screens first remove large objects such as rags and plastic.','1 mark: then, in settling tanks, solid particles sink and are removed as sludge.','1 mark: then aerobic bacteria in aerated tanks break down dissolved organic matter, so less oxygen is used up when the water is released. Disinfection to kill pathogens may be credited in place of any one of these steps.'])
threats=[
 ('06','Climate change','Climate change can move the suitable temperature and rainfall conditions away from the place where a species lives.','If the species cannot move, adapt or reproduce fast enough, its survival and population size fall and its risk of extinction rises.','A species may be unable to move because a barrier or a gap in the habitat blocks the way. Different species respond differently, and some ranges expand while others shrink.\n\nA species is endangered when it is at serious risk of extinction. It is extinct when no living members remain anywhere. The disappearance of a species from one area is not extinction if it survives elsewhere.',
  'Explain how climate change can cause a species to become endangered.','Conditions can move outside the range the species can tolerate. If it cannot reach suitable habitat or reproduce successfully, its numbers fall and its risk of extinction rises.',['1 mark: when temperature or rainfall changes, conditions move outside the range the species can tolerate.','1 mark: if it cannot move or adapt fast enough, its survival and reproduction fall, so the effect is that its numbers decrease and it becomes endangered.'],
  'A mountain species needs cool habitat. Warming has pushed it uphill, but it now lives at the summit and there is no higher cool area. Explain why moving uphill, which helped before, can no longer protect it.','As warming continues, the summit itself becomes too warm for the species, and there is no higher, cooler land to move to. Its survival and reproduction then fall.',['1 mark: as warming continues, the summit becomes too warm for this species and there is no higher land to move to.','1 mark: because it cannot escape the warming, its survival and breeding fall, so the population declines.']),
 ('07','Habitat destruction as a cause of endangerment','Habitat destruction removes food, shelter and breeding sites, or splits up the habitat so that animals cannot move and find mates.','Lower survival and breeding, and isolation, can shrink populations and raise the risk of extinction.','Follow the chain from the habitat lost to the resource or breeding site that has gone, and then to the effect on the population. Losing one patch can make a species disappear locally while other populations survive. Extinction means the loss of the species everywhere.\n\nConservation has to protect the particular habitat that the species needs. A large area of green cover is not enough if it lacks the feeding area or breeding site the species uses.',
  'Explain how habitat destruction can cause a species to become endangered or extinct.','Removing the food or breeding habitat reduces survival and reproduction. If all populations decline until no individuals remain anywhere, the species becomes extinct.',['1 mark: when habitat is destroyed, the species loses essential food, shelter or breeding sites.','1 mark: survival and reproduction decrease, so the effect is that the population shrinks; if no individuals remain anywhere, the species is extinct.'],
  'A wetland species has plenty of food in nearby grassland, but drainage destroys the only pools where it breeds. Explain why plentiful food does not prevent its decline.','Without the pools the species cannot breed, so no young are produced. Adults that die are not replaced, and the population declines however much food there is.',['1 mark: without the pools, this species cannot breed, so no young are produced.','1 mark: adults that die are not replaced, so the population decreases however much food there is.']),
 ('08','Pollution as a cause of endangerment','Poisons, loss of oxygen or physical waste can reduce the survival and breeding success of organisms.','A species with a small population can become endangered when pollution affects much of its remaining habitat.','Name a mechanism that matches the pollutant. Nutrient enrichment can cause low oxygen, a pesticide can poison organisms that were not its target, and a plastic loop can entangle an animal. A fall in numbers near a polluted site suggests a link, but other causes must be considered before concluding that pollution is responsible.',
  'Explain how severe nutrient pollution can threaten a species that lives in water.','Excess nutrients cause rapid growth of algae and plants. When they die, decomposers break down the dead material, and their aerobic respiration uses up dissolved oxygen. If the dissolved oxygen falls below what the species needs, survival and breeding fall and the population may become endangered.',['1 mark: excess nutrients increase the growth and then death of algae and plants, so decomposers use more oxygen.','1 mark: when dissolved oxygen falls below what the species needs, survival and breeding decrease, with the effect that its numbers decline.'],
  'Two streams have the same amount of fish food. One receives organic waste and often has very low dissolved oxygen. Explain why extra food would not prevent fish declining in that stream.','Microorganisms breaking down the organic waste use up the dissolved oxygen. Fish need oxygen for respiration, so they cannot survive there even though food is available.',['1 mark: in this stream, microorganisms breaking down the organic waste use up the dissolved oxygen.','1 mark: fish need oxygen for respiration, so they cannot survive there whatever the food supply, because oxygen, not food, is limiting.']),
 ('09','Hunting and poaching','Removing animals faster than the population can replace them reduces its size, and killing breeding adults reduces future births.','Illegal killing can threaten populations that are already small and can reduce income from tourism.','Poaching is illegal killing or taking of wildlife. A population can fall even when its habitat is intact, if the number of adults lost is greater than the number of young produced. Slow-breeding animals such as rhinoceros recover slowly because each female produces only a single calf at long intervals.\n\nIn north-west Namibia, local people help to monitor and protect the desert-adapted black rhinoceros. Information on exactly where threatened animals live is kept confidential, because it could help poachers.',
  'Explain how excessive hunting or poaching can endanger a species.','Deaths exceed the number of young produced. The loss of breeding adults then reduces future births, so the population gets smaller and recovers more slowly.',['1 mark: when deaths from hunting or poaching are greater than births, the population decreases.','1 mark: because breeding adults are killed, fewer young are born, so the effect is that the population recovers slowly and may become endangered.'],
  'A model animal population produces five surviving young a year but loses eight breeding adults a year to poaching, with all other changes balanced. Explain why having a stable habitat does not mean the population is stable.','The removal of eight is greater than the five young added, so the population falls by three a year. Protecting the habitat does not stop this loss of adults.',['1 mark: uses the given figures: 8 adults lost and 5 young added gives a net fall of 3 a year.','1 mark: habitat protection does not stop these deaths, so the population still declines.'])]
for n,h,c1,c2,more,p,a,g,ap,aa,ag in threats:
 o='5.4.2-'+n;E(o,h,c1,c2,more,loc='47.3 Threats to Biodiversity')
 C(o,'CHAIN',p,a,g)
 C(o,'APP',ap,aa,ag)
measures=[
 ('10','Monitoring and protecting species','Regular monitoring estimates population trends and finds the threats, so protection can be targeted and its success checked.','Protection can reduce harmful killing or disturbance, but a rising count is not proof of recovery if the amount of observation has changed.','Use the same places, times and effort in each survey, and record the uncertainty. A species can be hard to find, so failing to see it on one visit does not show that it is extinct.\n\nWorked example. A count rises from 12 to 20 animals, but the number of survey days rises from 2 to 10. The animals seen per survey day fell from 12 ÷ 2 = 6 to 20 ÷ 10 = 2. The extra effort explains the higher count and the real trend may be a decline. Always compare counts for equal effort.',
  'Describe how monitoring can support the protection of an endangered species.','Records of numbers and threats are collected using the same method and effort each time. Protection is then targeted at the threats found, so later counts show whether it has worked.',['1 mark: records of numbers and threats are collected using the same method and effort each time.','1 mark: protection is then targeted at the threats found, so later counts show whether it has worked.'],
  'A count rises from 12 to 20 animals while the survey effort rises from two to ten days. Explain why this does not show that the population is recovering.','The extra survey effort can explain the higher count, because more days give more sightings. Animals seen per day fell from 6 to 2, so the counts must be compared for equal effort.',['1 mark: calculates animals seen per survey day from the given figures, 12 ÷ 2 = 6 before and 20 ÷ 10 = 2 after.','1 mark: the count per day has fallen, so the higher total is explained by more survey effort and does not show recovery.']),
 ('11','Protecting habitats','Protecting suitable habitat preserves the food, shelter and breeding conditions that an endangered population needs.','Connected habitat and well-managed land around a reserve help animals to move and reduce isolation. A legal boundary works only if it is enforced.','A fenced reserve that cuts off a seasonal water source may fail even if it is large. The quality and position of the habitat matter as well as its size. Working with local communities and checking the real results helps long-term management.\n\nA reserve does not protect against everything. Disease, climate change and pollution can cross its boundary. Etosha National Park shows how important water is: many animals gather at the waterholes in the dry season, so the water supply is part of the habitat that needs protecting.',
  'Describe how habitat protection can help to conserve an endangered species.','The food, shelter and breeding habitat the species needs is kept or restored. Connected areas then let animals move and find mates, so survival and breeding improve.',['1 mark: the food, shelter and breeding habitat the species needs is kept or restored.','1 mark: connected areas then let animals move and find mates, so survival and breeding improve.'],
  'A reserve contains dry-season grazing but excludes the only dry-season water source. Explain why it may fail to conserve a herbivore.','In the dry season the herbivores cannot reach water, which they need to survive. Grazing alone does not meet all their habitat needs, so they may leave or die.',['1 mark: in the dry season the herbivores in this reserve cannot reach water, which they need to survive.','1 mark: grazing alone does not meet all their habitat needs, so they may leave or die and the reserve fails to conserve them.']),
 ('12','Education and conservation','Education can explain a conservation problem, its causes and what people can do to reduce it.','Changed behaviour can reduce threats, but awareness is not enough if people have no practical alternatives or incentives.','A useful education programme has a clear audience, an action that people can really take and a way to measure whether behaviour or the environment has changed. The number of posters displayed counts activity, not success. Respect local knowledge and people\'s livelihoods rather than assuming that people harm wildlife only because they lack information. In Namibia, conservancies pair training with real benefits, such as income from wildlife tourism.',
  'Describe how education can contribute to conserving an endangered species.','People are taught about the specific threat and an action they can take to reduce it. If they then adopt the action, the pressure on the species falls, so survival and breeding improve.',['1 mark: people are taught about the specific threat and an action they can take to reduce it.','1 mark: if they then adopt the action, harm to the species decreases, so its survival and breeding improve.'],
  'A community has no waste collection. Explain why many anti-litter posters may not reduce the waste in the river.','Without collection, people have nowhere to put the waste, so they cannot act on the message. Education works only when people have a practical alternative.',['1 mark: with no collection, people in this community have nowhere to put waste, so they cannot act on the message.','1 mark: education works only when there is a practical alternative, so posters alone may not reduce the waste in the river.']),
 ('13','Captive breeding','Captive breeding can increase the number of a threatened species and keep genetic variety through managed choice of parents.','Release can help wild recovery when suitable habitat exists and the threats are controlled, but breeding in captivity alone does not secure a population in the wild.','Breeding programmes need healthy animals, good welfare and care to avoid mating close relatives. Animals also need survival skills before they are let out, and disease risks must be managed. A large captive population can hide low genetic variety.\n\nReleasing animals into a place where the original threat continues, such as poaching or habitat loss, is likely to fail. To judge a programme, check the survival and breeding of released animals, not just how many were released.',
  'Describe how a captive-breeding programme can support conservation.','Animals are bred in captivity, with parents chosen to keep genetic variety and avoid inbreeding. Young animals are then released into secure, suitable habitat, so the wild population can increase.',['1 mark: animals are bred in captivity, with parents chosen to keep genetic variety and avoid inbreeding.','1 mark: offspring are then released into secure, suitable habitat, so the wild population can increase.'],
  'Many captive-bred animals are released into the same unprotected habitat where their parents were lost. Explain why this may fail to restore the population.','The released animals face the same threat, such as poaching or habitat loss, so many die before they breed. Adding animals does not remove the cause of the decline.',['1 mark: released animals in this habitat face the same threat, such as poaching or habitat loss, so many die before they breed.','1 mark: adding animals does not remove the cause of the decline, so the threat must be controlled first.']),
 ('14','Seed banks','Seed banks conserve seeds of suitable plants under controlled conditions, check their viability from time to time, and keep them for future restoration or breeding.','The collection should represent the genetic variety of the species. Some seeds cannot be kept by standard dry, cold storage and need other methods.','Seeds are collected in a way that does not remove too many from wild populations. Storage slows deterioration, but seeds do not stay viable for ever, so a sample is germinated regularly to test viability and stocks are renewed when needed.\n\nA seed bank keeps the potential for new plants. It does not restore a habitat, so suitable places to grow the plants are still needed.',
  'Describe how a seed bank helps to conserve a threatened plant.','Seeds are collected from many plants so that they represent the genetic variety. They are stored dry and cold to slow deterioration, and samples are germinated from time to time to test viability, so stocks can be renewed.',['1 mark each for any two of these steps: seeds are collected from many parent plants, so they represent the genetic variety; they are stored dry and cold to slow deterioration; samples are germinated from time to time to test viability, so stocks can be renewed.'],
  'A seed bank stores a thousand seeds from a single parent plant. Explain why this does not necessarily conserve the genetic variety of the whole plant population.','Seeds from one parent carry only that plant\'s genes, which are a small part of the variety in the population. Seeds should be collected from many parents, because the number of seeds is not the same as genetic variety.',['1 mark: seeds from this single parent carry only that plant\'s genes, a small part of the variety in the population.','1 mark: genetic variety needs seeds collected from many parent plants, so a large number of seeds is not the same as a varied collection.'])]
for n,h,c1,c2,more,p,a,g,ap,aa,ag in measures:
 o='5.4.2-'+n;E(o,h,c1,c2,more,loc='47.4 Preserving Biodiversity' if n in ('10','11','12','13') else None)
 C(o,'PROC',p,a,g)
 C(o,'APP',ap,aa,ag)
E('5.4.2-15','Tourism and conservation','Well-managed tourism can bring income and jobs that are linked to conserving wildlife and habitats, and can help to pay for protection and local livelihoods.',
 'Too many visitors, new buildings, waste and disturbance can damage habitats if tourism is poorly managed, so the benefits depend on good planning and a fair sharing of income.',
 'Namibia attracts visitors to its national parks and to the Namib Desert. In its communal conservancies, local people can earn an income from wildlife tourism, which gives them a reason to protect wildlife.\n\nTo evaluate tourism, give a benefit and a cost and then make a judgement. The judgement should say when the benefit outweighs the cost, for example when income is used to protect the wildlife and the number of visitors is controlled.\n\nWorked example. A reserve earns N$120 for each visitor, and nesting birds breed successfully only when there are 40 or fewer visitors a day. With 80 visitors a day the income is 80 × 120 = N$9600 a day, but the birds will breed poorly. A cap of 40 earns N$4800 a day and protects the birds. Choosing the higher income would harm the wildlife the visitors come to see.')
C('5.4.2-15','EVAL','Discuss an advantage and a disadvantage of tourism for conservation, with an overall judgement.','Tourism can pay for protection and give local jobs, but unmanaged visitors can disturb wildlife and damage habitat. On balance it supports conservation when the income benefits protection and communities and the visitor pressure is controlled.',['1 mark: a developed advantage, such as income that pays for protection and gives local people jobs.','1 mark: a developed disadvantage, such as visitors disturbing wildlife or damaging habitat.','1 mark: an overall judgement that says on balance tourism helps when it is managed and the income is used for conservation.'])
C('5.4.2-15','APP','A model reserve earns N$120 per visitor for conservation, but nesting success falls when there are more than 40 visitors a day. Option X allows 80 visitors a day and option Y allows 40 at the same fee. Suggest why the higher daily income of option X is not enough reason to choose it.','Option X earns N$9600 a day compared with N$4800 for Y, but it goes beyond the nesting limit of 40 visitors. Poor breeding would harm the wildlife that tourism depends on, so income must be judged together with the biological cost.',['1 mark: calculates the daily income from the given fee, N$9600 for X and N$4800 for Y, and notes that X\'s 80 visitors exceed the nesting limit of 40.','1 mark: because nesting success falls above 40 visitors, X would harm the birds that tourism depends on, so income alone is not enough reason to choose it.'])
E('5.4.2-16','Reasons for conservation programmes','Conservation programmes protect biodiversity and the natural processes that people depend on, and they support cultural values and sustainable livelihoods.',
 'A programme needs a defined threat, a realistic aim and monitoring, so that its results can be told apart from the activity involved.',
 'Pollination, water regulation and soil formation are examples of processes that support people and other organisms. A threatened species can also have value that does not depend on immediate profit. Different reasons can apply at once, and a programme should consider who gains and who bears the costs.\n\n"Save nature" is an aim. To justify a programme, explain the mechanism: which pressure is reduced and how survival or habitat condition then improves.',loc='47.4 Preserving Biodiversity')
C('5.4.2-16','CHAIN','Explain two linked reasons to conserve biodiversity through a planned programme.','Loss of species can disrupt processes on which other organisms and people depend. Protecting diverse, working ecosystems therefore preserves resources and livelihoods for the future.',['1 mark: if species are lost, ecological processes such as pollination, water regulation or soil formation can be disrupted.','1 mark: because these processes support food, water and income, protecting biodiversity has the effect of keeping these benefits for the future.'])
C('5.4.2-16','APP','A model orchard can buy replacement trees, but the habitat of its pollinating insects is being removed. Explain why protecting that habitat may help fruit production more than planting more trees alone.','Fruit forms only after flowers are pollinated, and the orchard depends on insects for this. Extra trees without pollinators will not produce more fruit, so protecting pollinator habitat helps production more.',['1 mark: fruit forms only after flowers are pollinated, and in this orchard the pollinating insects are losing their habitat.','1 mark: without pollinators, extra trees will not set more fruit, so protecting the insects\' habitat helps production more.'])
E('5.4.2-17','Investigating poaching and tourism','An ethical investigation can compare authorised records of poaching with measures of tourism, such as visits, bookings or income, over matched periods and at comparable sites.',
 'Other causes of change in tourism, and differences in how the records were made, must be considered. An association does not by itself prove that poaching caused the change.',
 'Do not interfere with poaching, reveal where animals live or ask people about illegal activity. Use public or authorised records without names or locations, and note any missing values. Define the area and time units clearly and compare similar seasons, because tourism changes with the time of year. Road closures, economic changes and new accommodation can also change visitor numbers.\n\nA clear table, repeated over several periods or sites, makes the pattern more reliable. A small observational study cannot rule out every other cause, so the conclusion should say what the data suggest, not that poaching has been proved to cause the change.')
C('5.4.2-17','SYNTH','Describe how you would investigate whether rhinoceros poaching is linked to tourism in Namibia, using authorised records that do not show the locations of animals.','Choose matched sites and periods with different recorded levels of poaching. Measure visits, bookings or income for each. Keep season, road access and recording effort the same, or record them as controls. Tabulate the anonymised authorised records, repeat across several periods or sites, and give other possible explanations instead of claiming proof that poaching is the cause.',['1 mark: names the independent variable, the recorded level of poaching, which is compared rather than changed by the investigator.','1 mark: names the dependent variable, a defined measure of tourism such as visits, bookings or income, which is measured for each site or period.','1 mark: keeps the season, access and recording effort the same, or controlled, between the periods compared.','1 mark: describes an ethical method, using authorised anonymised data in a table without the locations of animals.','1 mark: repeats the comparison over several periods or sites and gives a conclusion that allows for other explanations.'])
C('5.4.2-17','LIM','A poaching and tourism comparison uses a holiday month before a fall in poaching and a low-season month afterwards. Suggest the error in this comparison and an improvement.','Tourism differs naturally between holiday and low-season months, so the season confounds the comparison. Compare matched seasons across several periods, or adjust for season.',['1 mark: the error is that tourism naturally differs between holiday and low-season months, so the season, not poaching, may explain the change.','1 mark: an improvement is to compare the same season in different years, over several periods, instead.'])
C('5.4.2-17','APP','Tourist bookings fell after recorded poaching increased, but the main access road closed at the same time. Suggest why this does not show how much of the fall was caused by poaching.','The road closure could reduce visits by itself. The two changes happened together, so the effect of poaching cannot be separated unless access is taken into account.',['1 mark: closing the main road in this period would reduce bookings on its own, so it is a second possible cause.','1 mark: because both changes happened at the same time, their effects cannot be separated, so the data cannot show how much was due to poaching.'])
A.notes['deviations'].append({'from':'5.4.2-17 investigation slot could imply experimental manipulation.','to':'Ethical observational design using authorised aggregated records, explanatory rather than manipulated independent variable.','why':'Poaching must not be induced, sensitive locations exposed or real data invented; retains genuine planning/method-evaluation C demand.'})

# ---------------- Whole-topic tasks ----------------
mcq='''Choose the correct option in each question. Each is worth 1 mark.
1. Which organism is a producer in a sunlit pond?
A. Photosynthetic alga
B. Fungus digesting dead leaves
C. Fish eating insects
D. Bacterium feeding on sewage
2. In grass → locust, what does the arrow show?
A. A locust attacks grass
B. Energy passes from grass to locust
C. Oxygen passes from locust to grass
D. Grass moves towards a locust
3. What is nitrification?
A. Nitrogen gas becoming usable compounds
B. Nitrate becoming nitrogen gas
C. Ammonium being converted through nitrite to nitrate
D. Plant roots absorbing nitrate
4. Which term describes the community of organisms together with the non-living environment and their interactions?
A. Population
B. Species
C. Community
D. Ecosystem
5. Why are fossil fuels non-renewable on a human timescale?
A. They form far more slowly than they are used
B. They contain no stored energy
C. Discovering a deposit recreates it
D. They cannot release carbon dioxide
6. Which statement describes a species that is extinct?
A. None seen during one visit
B. None remains alive anywhere
C. The population is endangered
D. One local patch is empty
7. What is a central aim of conservation?
A. Maximum extraction now
B. Keeping one captive animal regardless of habitat
C. Maintaining biodiversity and supporting resources
D. Replacing varied ecosystems with one crop
8. In a stated budget, 700 kJ is eaten, 200 kJ is egested and 450 kJ is transferred to the surroundings through respiration. How much energy remains in new biomass?
A. 650 kJ
B. 500 kJ
C. 250 kJ
D. 50 kJ
9. A closed model population has 18 births and 25 deaths in one year. What is the net change?
A. Decrease of seven
B. Increase of seven
C. Increase of 43
D. No change
10. Recorded sightings of an animal double while the survey hours triple. Which inference is justified?
A. The population certainly doubled
B. Sightings per survey hour must be compared before claiming recovery
C. No animals remain
D. Survey effort is irrelevant
11. A chemical kills algae immediately without adding nutrients. Which explanation fits the evidence most directly?
A. A fertiliser-driven algal bloom has occurred
B. Nitrogen fixation must have stopped
C. Direct poisoning is likely and nutrient enrichment is not shown
D. All chemical pollution works in the same way
12. Grain stocks in the market are unchanged, but many households cannot afford to buy grain. Which cause of famine does this show?
A. Drought lowering the harvest
B. Flooding of the grain stores
C. A larger population sharing a fixed harvest
D. Poverty limiting people's ability to buy available food
'''
keys=[
 ('A','The alga makes organic food by photosynthesis.','B is a decomposer, C is a consumer and D is a decomposer feeding on waste, which is the common confusion between producers and decomposers.'),
 ('B','The arrow points towards the organism that receives the energy.','A reads the arrow as an attack, C reverses what is transferred and D reads the arrow as movement of the organism.'),
 ('C','Nitrifying bacteria convert ammonium through nitrite to nitrate.','A describes fixation, B describes denitrification and D describes uptake by plants.'),
 ('D','An ecosystem includes the organisms and their non-living environment, interacting.','A is one species in one area, B is a kind of organism, and C leaves out the non-living part.'),
 ('A','Fossil fuels form over millions of years, far more slowly than they are used.','B denies that they store energy, C confuses discovery with formation and D denies that burning releases carbon dioxide.'),
 ('B','Extinction means that no living member of the species remains anywhere.','A is limited detection, C describes a species that still has living members and D is a local loss only.'),
 ('C','Conservation maintains biodiversity and the resources that support it.','A ignores the future, B ignores the habitat and population and D reduces diversity.'),
 ('D','700 − 200 − 450 = 50 kJ.','A adds the outgoing amounts, B subtracts only the egested energy and C subtracts only the respiration.'),
 ('A','18 − 25 = −7, so there are seven fewer individuals.','B reverses the direction, C adds births and deaths and D ignores the unequal numbers.'),
 ('B','When sightings double while hours triple, sightings per hour fall to two thirds of their earlier value, so the rise in the total count does not show recovery.','A ignores the effort, C contradicts the sightings and D ignores that more hours give more chances to see animals.'),
 ('C','Poisoning is the direct explanation when no nutrients were added.','A needs added nutrients, B has no link to the evidence and D ignores the differences between pollutants.'),
 ('D','If prices are high or incomes low, households cannot buy the grain that is available.','A, B and C each describe a change in the supply of food, but the stocks have not changed.')]
A.task('multiple_choice_set',['5.1-02','5.1-09','5.1-04','5.2-03','5.3-04','5.4.2-02','5.4.2-07','5.4.2-01','5.1-12','5.3-02','5.4.2-10','5.4.1-15','5.4.1-10'],mcq,
 '\n'.join(f'{n}. {k}. {why} Distractors: {wrong}' for n,(k,why,wrong) in enumerate(keys,1)),
 ['Award 1 mark for each correct option (key), 12 marks in total.','No mark is given for a distractor, or when more than one option is chosen.'],12,['AO1','AO2'])
A.task('short_answer',['5.4.1-14','5.4.1-15','5.1-02','5.4.1-17'],'''Answer all parts. After heavy rain carries excess crop fertiliser into a model lake, algal cover increases. Later, dead material builds up and the dissolved oxygen falls to 3 mg/L from 7 mg/L. The fish need at least 5 mg/L, and their food is still plentiful.
(a) Name one fertiliser nutrient that can stimulate the first algal growth. [1]
(b) State the trophic role of the photosynthetic algae. [1]
(c) Describe how an increase in dead organic matter can lower the dissolved oxygen. [2]
(d) Explain why fish may die despite plentiful food, using the information given. [2]
(e) Suggest two farm practices that could reduce nutrient loss to the lake without leaving the crop short of nutrients. [2]''',
 '''(a) Nitrate or phosphate. (b) Producer.
(c) Decomposers feed on the dead material, and their aerobic respiration uses up the dissolved oxygen.
(d) The oxygen concentration of 3 mg/L is below the 5 mg/L the fish need, so they cannot respire enough to survive. Plentiful food does not supply the missing oxygen.
(e) Match the amount and timing of fertiliser to the crop and soil needs; leave a strip of vegetation between the field and the water to trap runoff. Any two distinct suitable practices.''',
 ['(a) 1 mark for nitrate or phosphate. (b) 1 mark for producer.',
 '(c) 2 marks: 1 mark for decomposers using the dead material, 1 mark for their aerobic respiration using up oxygen.',
 '(d) 2 marks: 1 mark for using the figures given, 3 mg/L is below the 5 mg/L needed, and 1 mark for the reason that plentiful food does not remove the shortage of oxygen.',
 '(e) 2 marks: 1 mark for each distinct, suitable practice that reduces nutrient runoff. There are 8 creditable points in total.'],8,['AO1','AO2'],command='name')
A.task('practical_task',['5.4.1-14','5.4.1-15'],'''A teacher sets up equal open transparent containers, each with the same volume of water and the same starting mass of a safe algal culture. The teacher changes the nitrate concentration in each container and leaves them for seven days. Final algal biomass is then estimated using a calibrated method.

| Nitrate concentration / mg/L | Biomass replicate 1 / mg | Biomass replicate 2 / mg | Mean biomass / mg |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 4 | 6 | 5 |
| 2 | 10 | 14 | ... |
| 4 | 17 | 19 | 18 |
| 8 | 18 | 20 | 19 |

(a) State the independent variable. [1]
(b) State the dependent quantity and its unit. [1]
(c) Calculate the missing mean. [2]
(d) Describe the trend in the data, including what happens from 4 to 8 mg/L. [2]
(e) State one controlled variable. [1]
(f) Suggest the error, and a linked improvement, if one container received much less light. [2]
(g) Suggest why this table alone cannot show how eutrophication kills fish. [1]''',
 '''(a) Nitrate concentration. (b) Final algal biomass, in mg.
(c) 10 + 14 = 24; 24 ÷ 2 = 12 mg.
(d) Mean biomass rises strongly from 0 to 4 mg/L; from 4 to 8 mg/L the rise is small, from 18 to 19 mg.
(e) Light intensity, temperature, starting biomass, water volume or time; any one.
(f) Unequal light would affect growth as well as nitrate, so the nitrate effect is confounded; give all containers the same light.
(g) The table shows only algal growth. It has no data on decomposition, dissolved oxygen or fish survival, so it cannot show the later stages.''',
 ['(a) 1 mark for the variable. (b) 1 mark for the measurement with its unit. (c) 1 mark for the working and 1 mark for 12 mg.',
 '(d) 1 mark for the strong rise at low concentrations and 1 mark for the small change from 4 to 8 mg/L, quoting the data. (e) 1 mark for one controlled variable.',
 '(f) 1 mark for the error of unequal light and 1 mark for the improvement of equal light. (g) 1 mark for the limitation that the method and table cover only algal growth, so the conclusion cannot include fish death. There are 10 marks in total.'],10,['AO3'],command='describe and explain')
A.notes['deviations'].append({'from':'Generic Topic 5 practical performance task.','to':'Teacher-provided safe algal microcosm readings assess the nutrient-growth stage, with an explicit limit on inferring the full eutrophication mechanism.','why':'Provides a complete practical interpretation task without sewage handling, induced wildlife harm or unsupported proof of fish deaths.'})
summaries={
 '5.1':'| Role or diagram | Key idea |\n|---|---|\n| Producer | makes organic nutrients, usually by photosynthesis |\n| Consumer | gets energy by feeding on other organisms |\n| Decomposer | gets energy from dead and waste organic material |\n| Food chain or web | one route, or linked routes, of energy transfer |\n| Pyramid of biomass | dry mass at each feeding level at one moment |\n\nEnergy enters most ecosystems as light and leaves as heat. Less energy is left at each feeding step because some material is not eaten, some is egested, and much is used in respiration and lost as heat, so chains are short. Arrows point from the food to the feeder. A pyramid of biomass shows mass at one moment, not the rate of energy flow.',
 '5.2':'| Nitrogen process | Organisms and conditions |\n|---|---|\n| Fixation | bacteria turn nitrogen gas into compounds |\n| Decomposition | decomposers release ammonium from dead material |\n| Nitrification | bacteria turn ammonium through nitrite into nitrate, with oxygen |\n| Denitrification | bacteria turn nitrate into nitrogen gas, without oxygen |\n\nCarbon moves in by photosynthesis, along food chains by feeding, and back to the air by respiration, decomposition and combustion. Fossilisation locks carbon away for a very long time. Matter cycles, but energy leaves as heat.',
 '5.3':'A population is one species in one area at one time. A community is all the populations in an ecosystem, and an ecosystem also includes the physical environment. Births and immigration add individuals, and deaths and emigration remove them. An S-shaped curve levels off when limiting factors act. On a graph, height shows size and slope shows the change per unit time. Human impact depends on the number of people, on what each person uses, and on how resources and waste are managed.',
 '5.4.1':'Food security depends on production, distribution and the ability to buy food. Drought, floods, population growth and poverty can each break one of those links. Monocultures and intensive farming reduce diversity or concentrate waste. Pollution can work by enrichment, by poison, by pathogens or by physical harm. Eutrophication runs from excess nutrients to algal growth, dead material, aerobic decomposition, low oxygen and fish deaths. Pollution is reduced by controlling its source, by recycling, and by matching fertiliser to crop need.',
 '5.4.2':'Conservation maintains biodiversity and the resources that support it. Sustainable development meets present needs while preserving the environment. Match the measure to the threat: habitat loss, killing, pollution or changing conditions. Monitoring must use equal effort. Captive breeding and seed banks keep the potential for recovery but need suitable habitat in the wild. Tourism can pay for protection but can also cause disturbance, so it must be managed.'}
links={
 '5.1':'Photosynthesis in 3.6.1 and respiration in 3.10 explain the energy and carbon movements in a food chain. The trophic roles you learn here underpin the cycles in 5.2.',
 '5.2':'Amino acids and proteins are covered in 3.4 and 3.6.1, and root uptake in 3.3.3. The equations for photosynthesis and respiration are in 3.6.1 and 3.10. Pollution pathways are developed in 5.4.1.',
 '5.3':'Ratios, percentage change and graph axes from Topic 1 are used here. The food resources of 5.1 and the nutrient supply of 5.2 limit population growth, and human pressures follow in 5.4.1.',
 '5.4.1':'Nitrate use by plants is in 3.6.1, aerobic respiration in 3.10 and microbial cycling in 5.2. Demand from larger populations is in 5.3, and conservation responses follow in 5.4.2.',
 '5.4.2':'Habitat and resource pressures from 5.3 and 5.4.1 explain why species become endangered. Inherited variation and selection are in 4.4.2 to 4.4.4, germination testing is in 4.3.5, and investigation controls and error are in Topic 1.'}
A.notes['visual_requirements_pending']=[{'objective_id':r['objective_id'],'requirement_id':r['requirement_id'],'reason':'Operator approval does not resolve exact-use scientific/model/variant/delivery acceptance; current holds remain.'} for r in json.loads((WS/'curriculum/visual-requirements.json').read_text())['requirements'] if r['objective_id'] in A.entries]
A.notes['scientific_judgements']=['Ecological and tourism scenarios and numerical readings are invented models, not current Namibian statistics.','Carbon-stock and annual-flow distinctions are explicit; more uptake alone is not balance.','Extinction cause explanations are taught independently; definition evidence is not represented as testing the causal route.','Human-population growth-rate item now carries its own data table; biomass and sigmoid figure items remain held.']
